\subsection{Ablation Study}
\label{sec:Ablation}
To validate the effectiveness of the Rescaling Mechanism, we set the following ablation configurations: (1) \textbf{Rs×2:} rescaling adjacent nodes and subgraph edges, i.e., RsGCN;
(2) \textbf{Rs×1:} only rescaling adjacent nodes;
(3) \textbf{Rs×0:} no rescaling;
(4) \textbf{5-NN:} Re2Opt with the 5-nearest neighbors as candidates.
Figure \ref{fig:Ablation} reveals that the Rescaling Mechanism is crucial for improving GCNs' generalization, where \textbf{5-NN} acts as the borderline and the gap to \textbf{5-NN} is presented. Without rescaling edges, \textbf{Rs×1} shows inferior generalization compared to \textbf{Rs×2}. Even worse, \textbf{Rs×0} fails to provide effective guidance for TSPs with 1K nodes and more. Due to its quadratic computational complexity, \textbf{Rs×0} fails to complete inference for TSP-5K/10K within GPU memory. Figure \ref{fig:Record} shows that the overall convergence speed follows \textbf{Rs×2} > \textbf{Rs×1} > \textbf{5-NN} > \textbf{Rs×0}, validating the efficient guidance of RsGCN (the y-axis represents the average tour length across 16 instances for each scale, and the x-axis represents the runtime). Furthermore, ablation study on Re2Opt is presented in Appendix \ref{app:AblaRe2Opt}.

