The \textit{Relocate} operator moves a sequence of nodes to a new position within the same route or a different route, while the \textit{Swap} operator exchanges two sequences of nodes. To clarify their implementations, these operations are categorized as \textit{Intra-Relocate}, \textit{Intra-Swap}, \textit{Inter-Relocate}, and \textit{Inter-Swap} depending on whether they operate within a single route or across two different routes. The \textit{2-opt*} operator, applied between two routes, breaks one edge in each route, exchanges the subsequences, and reconnects them to form new routes. In contrast, the \textit{2-opt} move operates within a single route by breaking two non-adjacent edges and reconnecting the reversed subsequence. Note that \textit{2-opt} is only suitable for problems with symmetric distance matrices and without directed constraints, such as time windows.

The tensor-based implementation for each operator follows a generic process composed of five steps: (1) Extract relevant parts of the solution tensor $\mathbf{T}_s$ according to operators. (2) Compute attribute tensors representing all possible concatenated sequences within the operator's neighborhood. (3) Compute the differences of attribute values between the original and modified routes. (4) Obtain the objective tensor $\mathbf{F}$ based on the objective function and identify the best-improvement value and the corresponding indices in the objective tensor $\mathbf{F}$ for the best move. (5) Update the solution $S$ and the solution tensor $\mathbf{T}_s$ based on the best move. The detailed description of these tensor-based implementation steps is given below. 

\subsubsection{Extraction}
\label{sec:extraction}

The extraction step is crucial for distinguishing between different operators. Building on the concatenation operation for move evaluation outlined in Section \ref{sec:move_eval}, this step involves extracting specific parts of the solution tensor $\mathbf{T}_s$ that represent the relevant subsequences of routes and contain all the corresponding attribute information needed to compute the attributes of the concatenated sequences in the subsequent steps.

Given the features of the tensor representation, we categorize the operators based on their application within the same route (referred to as intra-operators) or between two routes (referred to as inter-operators). Intra-operators include \textit{Intra-Relocate}, \textit{Intra-Swap}, and \textit{2-opt}, while inter-operators consist of \textit{Inter-Relocate}, \textit{Inter-Swap}, and \textit{2-opt*}. This distinction allows for customized processing based on operator type.

\begin{align}
    \label{eq:2_opt}
    &\mathbf{S}_h = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::1l}, \quad l \in [1, K-4] \\
    &\mathbf{S}_m = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::kl}, \quad k \in [2,K-3], l \in [3, K-2],  \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_t = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:jkl}, \quad j \in [1, J], k \in [4, K-1], l = |R_j| \nonumber
\end{align}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/2_opt.jpg}
    \caption{An example of the extraction operation for \textit{2-opt} and the reshape operation. We show a 3D tensor with dimensions $J \times K \times K = 2 \times 10 \times 10$, omitting the first dimension of $\mathbf{T}_s$.}
    \label{fig:ext_2_opt}
\end{figure}

\textit{Extraction for intra-operators:} The intra-operators (\textit{Intra-Relocate}, \textit{Intra-Swap} and \textit{2-opt}) involve only a single route, so we propose a route-based extraction for these operators. The \textit{2-opt} operation divides the route into three subsequences: the head sequence $\mathbf{S}_h$, the middle sequence $\mathbf{S}_m$, and the tail sequence $\mathbf{S}_t$. The middle sequence contains the sequence to be reversed, the head sequence consists of the nodes preceding the middle sequence while the tail sequence includes the nodes following it. This extraction process can be described by Equation (\ref{eq:2_opt}) and illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:ext_2_opt}. Considering that the \textit{2-opt} operator is only applied to the variants with symmetric settings, the reverse of the middle sequence can be achieved by swapping the first and last node indices of the middle sequence. To ensure proper concatenation in the following steps, the extracted tensors must be appropriately reshaped. This reshaping process is applied consistently to all subsequent operators as well.


The \textit{Intra-Relocate} operation divides the route into four subsequences: the head sequence $S_h$, the sequence to be relocated $S_r$, the destination sequence $S_d$, and the tail sequence $S_t$. There are two cases to consider based on the relative positions of $S_r$ and $S_d$: either $S_r$ precedes $S_d$ or follows it. In both cases, the head and tail sequences remain unchanged. To optimize computational efficiency, we merge sequences $S_r$ and $S_d$ to address both cases simultaneously. The complete extraction process for \textit{Intra-Relocate} is outlined in Equation (\ref{eq:ext_intra_relocate}). Figure \ref{fig:ext_intra_relocate} provides a visual example of the extraction process for the \textit{Intra-Relocate} operator.

\begin{align}
\label{eq:ext_intra_relocate}
&\mathbf{S}_h = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::1l}, \quad l \in [1, K-N-2] \\
&\mathbf{S}_p = \text{merge}(\mathbf{S}_r^1, \mathbf{S}_d^2) \nonumber\\
&\mathbf{S}_q = \text{merge}(\mathbf{S}_d^1, \mathbf{S}_r^2) \nonumber\\
&\mathbf{S}_t = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:jkl}, \quad j \in [1, J], k \in [N+3, K], l = |R_j| \nonumber \\
\text{s.t.} \qquad &\mathbf{S}_r^1 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N-1)}, \quad k \in [2, K-N-1] \nonumber\\
&\mathbf{S}_d^2 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::kl}, \quad k, l \in [2, K-N-1]\nonumber \\
&\mathbf{S}_d^1 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::kl}, \quad k, l \in [N+2, K-1] \nonumber \\
&\mathbf{S}_r^2 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N-1)}, \quad k \in [3, K-N] \nonumber
\end{align}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/ext_intra_relocate.jpg}
    \caption{An example of the extraction operation for \textit{Intra-Relocate} with $N=2$ and the reshape operation. We give only a 3D tensor with dimensions $J \times K \times K = 1 \times 10 \times 10$ to show its complicated extracted parts, omitting the first dimension of $\mathbf{T}_s$ and setting $J=1$.}
    \label{fig:ext_intra_relocate}
\end{figure}
Similarly, the \textit{Intra-Swap} operation divides the routes into five subsequences: the head sequence $S_h$, the sequence to be swapped $S_p$ (of length $N_1$), the fixed sequence $S_m$, the second sequence to be swapped $S_q$ (of length $N_2$), and the tail sequence $S_t$.

\begin{align}
    \label{eq:ext_intra_swap}
    &\mathbf{S}_h = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::1l}, \quad l \in [1, K-N_1-N_2-2] \\
    &\mathbf{S}_p = \text{merge}(\mathbf{S}_r^1, \mathbf{S}_d^2) \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_m = \text{merge}(\mathbf{S}_m^1, \mathbf{S}_m^2) \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_q = \text{merge}(\mathbf{S}_d^1, \mathbf{S}_r^2) \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_t = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:jkl}, \quad j \in [1, J], k \in [N_1+N_2+3, K], l = |R_j| \nonumber \\
    \text{s.t.} \qquad &\mathbf{S}_r^1 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N_1-1)}, \quad k \in [2, K-N_1-N_2-1] \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_d^2 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N_2-1)}, \quad k \in [2, K-N_1-N_2-1] \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_m^1 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::kl}, \quad k, l \in [N_1+2, K-N_2-1] \nonumber \\
    &\mathbf{S}_m^2 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::kl}, \quad k, l \in [N_2+2, K-N_1-1] \nonumber \\
    &\mathbf{S}_d^1 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N_2-1)}, \quad k \in [N_1+3, K-N_2] \nonumber \\
    &\mathbf{S}_r^2 = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N_1-1)}, \quad k \in [N_2+3, K-N_1] \nonumber
\end{align}
When the lengths of the two sequences differ, there are two cases to consider. A merging process is also  applied to optimize the extraction. The complete extraction process for \textit{Intra-Swap} is detailed in Equation (\ref{eq:ext_intra_swap}). 
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/ext_intra_swap.jpg}
    \caption{An example of the extraction operation for \textit{Intra-Swap} with $N_1=1, N_2=2$ and the reshape operation. We give only a 3D tensor with dimensions $J \times K \times K = 1 \times 10 \times 10$ to show its complicated extracted parts, omitting the first dimension of $\mathbf{T}_s$ and setting $J=1$.}
    \label{fig:ext_intra_swap}
\end{figure}
Figure \ref{fig:ext_intra_swap} illustrates the extraction process for the \textit{Intra-Swap} operator.


\textit{Extraction for inter-operators:} Building on the same principle used for intra-operators, we extend the route-based extraction approach to inter-operators (\textit{Inter-Relocate}, \textit{Inter-Swap}, and \textit{2-opt*}). These operators share a similar schema, implemented using the so-called 3-Seq operation. That is, we split the routes into a head sequence $\mathbf{S}_h$, a middle sequence $\mathbf{S}_m$, and a tail sequence $\mathbf{S}_t$, while its middle sequence $\mathbf{S}_m$ represents the sequence to be relocated or swapped. The route-based 3-Seq($N$) operation is described by Equation (\ref{eq:inter_three_seq}), where $N$ represents the length of the sequence to be relocated or swapped. Note that the middle sequence $\mathbf{S}_m$ is empty when $N=0$, which occurs in the \textit{2-opt*} and \textit{Inter-Relocate} operators. Figure \ref{fig:ext_3_seq} illustrates the route-based implementation of the 3-Seq operation.

\begin{align}
    \label{eq:inter_three_seq}
    &\mathbf{S}_h = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::1l}, \quad l \in [1, K-N-1], N \ge 0 \\
    &\mathbf{S}_m = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{::k(k+N-1)} \text{ for } N > 0, k \in [2, K-N]; \, \emptyset \text{ for } N = 0. \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_t = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:jkl}, \quad j \in [1, J], k \in [N+2, K], l = |R_j|, N \ge 0 \nonumber 
\end{align}
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/3_seq.jpg}
    \caption{An example of the route-based implementation of 3-Seq operation with $N=1$ for inter-operators and reshape operation. Here we show a 3D tensor with dimensions $J \times K \times K = 2 \times 10 \times 10$, omitting the first dimension of $\mathbf{T}_s$ for clarity. Note that the length of the routes affects the tail sequence $\mathbf{S}_t$.}
    \label{fig:ext_3_seq}
\end{figure}

It is worth noting that the route-based implementation of the 3-Seq operation can result in high-dimensional intermediate and objective tensors, along with increased computational complexity in subsequent steps, because the inter-operators involves the computation between routes. A detailed complexity analysis is provided in Section \ref{sec:complexity}. To address these challenges, we propose a more efficient alternative: the node-based implementation of the 3-Seq operation. In this approach, we first extract the positional information $\mathbf{P} = [\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{I}]$ from the solution tensor $\mathbf{T}_s$, where $\mathbf{R}$ indicates the route index of each valid node (including depot nodes and customer nodes), and $\mathbf{I}$ denotes the node's position within its route. Using this positional information, we then extract the head sequence $\mathbf{S}_h$, the middle sequence $\mathbf{S}_m$, and the tail sequence $\mathbf{S}_t$ for all valid nodes. The node-based implementation is formalized in Equation (\ref{eq:node_based}), and an illustrative example is shown in Figure \ref{fig:node_based}. The key distinction between the two implementations is that the route-based method extracts subsequences based on the route structure, which may include invalid elements due to the maximum route length value $K$, whereas the node-based method focuses on valid nodes using positional information, resulting in greater computational efficiency. 

Using the 3-Seq operation, we can accurately define the extraction process for the inter-operators. The \textit{Inter-Relocate} operator involves a 3-Seq($N$) ($N > 0$) operation and a 3-Seq($0$) operation. The \textit{Inter-Swap} operator requires two separate operations: 3-Seq($N_1$) and 3-Seq($N_2$) ($N_1, N_2 > 0$). The \textit{2-opt*} operator relies solely on 3-Seq($0$) operations.

To summarize, in the standard implementation of TGA, intra-operators use route-based extraction, while inter-operators adopt the node-based implementation of the 3-Seq operation. 


\begin{align}
    \label{eq:node_based}
    &\mathbf{S}_h = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:\mathbf{R}1(\mathbf{I}-1)}, \text{ for } N > 0; (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:\mathbf{R}1\mathbf{I}} \text{ for } N = 0. \\
    &\mathbf{S}_m = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:\mathbf{R}\mathbf{I}(\mathbf{I}+N-1)} \text{ for } N > 0; \emptyset \text{ for } N = 0. \nonumber\\
    &\mathbf{S}_t = (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{I}+N)\mathbf{L}_\mathbf{R}}, \text{ for } N > 0; (\mathbf{T}_s)_{:\mathbf{R}(\mathbf{I}+1)\mathbf{L}_\mathbf{R}} \text{ for } N = 0, \mathbf{L} = \{|R_i|, |R_2|, ..., |R_J|\}. \nonumber 
\end{align}
