\section{Degree and Sensitivity of Symmetric Functions}\label{appendix}
We give a short argument that for a symmetric Boolean function $f$, both $\mathsf{deg}(f)$ and $\mathsf{s}(f)$ are both at least $n/2$. 

For the sensitivity lower bound, we observe that there exists some $\ell \in [n]$ such that for every $z,z'\in \{0,1\}^n$ with $\|z\|_1 =\|z'\|_1 +1 =\ell$, we have $f(z) \neq f(z')$. If $\|z'\|_1 <  n/2$, then flipping any coordinate $i$ with $z'_i = 0$ flips the function value. Otherwise, if $\|z'\|_1 \geq  n/2$ then flipping any coordinate $i$ with $z_i = 1$ flips the function value. Either way, at least $n/2$ coordinates can be flipped.

Now, we lower bound the degree of $f$. First, assume without loss of generality that \begin{align}\label{eq:f-zeros}
|\{\ell\in[n]: f(x) = 0 \text{ and } \|x\|_1 = \ell\}| \geq \lceil n/2\rceil ,
\end{align} 
otherwise, we can work with the function $1-f$, whose degree is the same. Using the multilinear expansion of $f$, we can write
\[f(x) = \sum_{S\subseteq [n]}c_S\prod_{i\in S}x_i.\] By the symmetry of $f$, it must be the case that $c_S = c_{S'}$ for all $|S| = |S'|$. Hence, on any input $x$ with Hamming weight $\ell$, $f(x)$ evaluates to
    \[\sum_{i=0}^nc_{[i]}\binom{\ell}{i}.\]

    The above expression implies that the univariate polynomial $\tilde f(y)$ given by 
    \[\tilde f(y) = \sum_i c_{[i]}\cdot \binom{y}{i}\] computes $f$.
    However, $\tilde f$ vanishes on at least half the points by \Cref{eq:f-zeros}. Therefore, the degree of $f$, which is the same as that of $\tilde f$ is at least $\lceil n/2\rceil$.
\fi
