\section{Introduction}

%While LLMs are advancing in base capabilities with each new release of foundation-class models, the amount of quality, energy aware training data in the domain of parallel codes remains limited. A future with sufficient training data remains challenging because the scale of specialists and users in this space may not reach the scale of serial developers and engineers who have fed the mass of code training data utilized by LLMs to date.  Therefore, we \emph{expect} that state-of-the-art LLMs today should present lesser capability in parallel codes compared to general, serial code applications. As we target one only programming framework in this study, CUDA C++, related LLM capability could be even lower. 

%Our hypothesis is that by harnessing an LLM within a guided framework for code generation, we can augment its base capability to produce valid solutions for a given task with higher frequency and consistency.

%It is well recognized that
Large energy demands in high-performance computing (HPC) systems have become a major concern in terms of stress on current power infrastructure and environmental impact from carbon emission \cite{silva_review_2024}.
Current exascale systems require between 24 and 39 MW of power \cite{Top500}.  Further, the hyperscale tech companies are seeking facilities that can support gigawatts of power, introducing challenges in expanding existing transmission networks \cite{Hyperscale}.
Therefore, seeking energy efficiency opportunities in all aspects of the HPC design stack, is essential, from the hardware stack to the software stack that delivers scientific solutions. This paper tackles the issue by focusing on energy efficiency of codes written for parallel scientific applications, specifically by generating these codes with the assistance of a large language model (LLM).

LLMs are now widely used for code generation, with recent attention on parallel, scientific code generation.  
In particular, existing work considers functionally correct parallel scientific code generation from a stand-alone LLM \cite{chaturvedi_hpc-coder-v2_2024}.  
While this is important, very little work has been done with performant parallel code generation, which focuses on execution time and power requirements, i.e., energy efficient code generation whereby the target execution platform must be considered.

In this paper, we propose \textbf{LASSI-EE} (\textbf{L}LM-based \textbf{A}utomated \textbf{S}elf-correcting pipeline for generating parallel \textbf{S}c\textbf{I}entific codes for \emph{Energy Efficiency}), an automated LLM-based refactoring pipeline framework. LASSI-EE achieves improved energy efficiency by revising existing parallel scientific codes through a multi-stage, self-correcting, iterative approach, taking into consideration the computing platform used for execution and evaluation.  Testing with 20 benchmark codes from the HeCBench suite \cite{HeCBench}, LASSI-EE optimized 85\% of these codes with an average energy reduction of 47\%.

The contributions of this paper include the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Propose LASSI-EE, an LLM-based automated and self-correcting pipeline for generating performant parallel scientific code with a focus on energy efficiency.
    \item Demonstrate the use of LASSI-EE using 20 benchmark codes from the HeCBench suite targeting the NVIDIA A100 system for execution.  The results demonstrate an average energy reduction of 47\%.
    \item Discuss how LASSI-EE can be extended to other parallel languages and compute architectures.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. A summary of related work is given in Section 2. Section 3 proposes the LASSI-EE automated pipeline framework and discusses its detailed design.  Section 4 presents our experimental results using LASSI-EE for 20 HeCBench codes executed on an NVIDIA A100 system. Section 5 discusses insights into LASSI-EE and its potential extensions.  Section 6 summarizes the paper and discusses some future work.
%The subsequent section discusses related work, followed by... %a description of LASSI-EE in \S 3.  Next, \S 4 outlines the benchmark codes and the four LLMs used for experimenting with LASSI, followed by the actual results presented in \S 5.   The paper summary is given in \S 6.

\section{Related Work}

LLMs are established as a powerhouse for general programming, with many models specialized for coding tasks \cite{hui2024qwen2, deepseek-ai_deepseek-coder-v2_2024, qwen, godoy_evaluation_2023, lozhkov_starcoder_2024, roziere_code_2024, Codestral2501}. This is largely made possible due to the extensive volume of publicly available codes accessible for building training corpora. However, in the specialized domain of parallel scientific codes, the prevalence of open repositories is significantly less, making it challenging to train models on sufficient, high-quality data necessary for a capable LLM. 

Studies of LLMs and parallel programs started early \cite{chen_evaluating_2021, godoy_evaluation_2023, Nichols_hpc-coder_2024}. These were followed by a critical evaluation from Nichols et al. that questioned if LLMs could excel in this area \cite{Nichols2024}, using a benchmark called \emph{ParEval} developed for evaluating parallel code generations. They extended a prior code generation performance metric, $pass@k$ \cite{chen_evaluating_2021} that estimates the probability generated code will provide a correct solution within $k$ attempts, to consider speedup and efficiency in the context of parallel frameworks. They found that while later models (up to GPT-4) were generating parallel codes with greater speedup compared to serial counterparts, they were not improving at maximizing these speedups. This result suggests that an LLM alone can write parallel code but not performant parallel code that utilizes the available compute resources in the best ways possible. %\mtd{If we want to follow-up with a deeper evaluation of the performance of codes generated through LASSI-EE, we can adapt the $speedup_n@k$ and $efficiency_n@k$ metrics introduced by Nichols et al. It requires a lot more data collection, so there is not enough time to drop in a quick evaluation with these here.}

A recent parallel code generation technique, specifically for science codes, is the Parallel Pattern Language (PPL), prototyped by Schmitz et al. \cite{schmitz_parallel_2024}. This framework allows HPC developers to start with reusable high-level parallel patterns on which the PPL applies compile-time global optimizations automatically through a custom Domain Specific Language (DSL). Then, a multi-step toolchain concludes by generating optimized C++ code targeting heterogeneous clusters. The LASSI-EE approach also attempts to introduce optimizations for parallel codes through an automated toolchain. However, our method takes this to the next level by leveraging an LLM for semantic code understanding, functional validation review, iterative self-correction, and the ability to implement energy-saving optimizations beyond what can be provided through the compiler.

Muller and Lou \cite{lou_automatic_2024} applied static resource analysis to automatically optimize CUDA kernels using the performance modeling tool RaCUDA \cite{muller_modeling_2021}. Here, candidate optimizations are tested without executing the code and the execution cost is predicted using static analysis. Their prototype tool, COpPER (CUDA Optimization through Programmatic Estimation of Resources), improved the performance of test kernels by 2--4\%. COpPER applies code transformations to improve performance, but these may not be energy-aware, as is the target goal of LASSI-EE. Also, LASSI-EE does not make predictions but takes real-time, direct measurements of power during runtime, which then becomes feedback to guide iterative code generation attempts, leading to performance gains potentially much higher than that attainable by COpPER. 

%\lan{add a paragraph to highlight the novelty of LASSI-EE. also it might be better to move this section after the result section.}
%In this work, the LASSI-EE pipeline introduces a novel approach to energy-aware parallel programming by being the first solution to leverage LLMs for automated code refactoring for improving energy efficiency. 
Unlike prior studies focused on correctness or static analysis, LASSI-EE uniquely integrates multiple techniques, including a self-prompting context mechanism, a self-correcting feedback loop, and an LLM-as-a-Judge agent for functional validation to deliver energy savings to existing parallel scientific codes. To the best of our knowledge, LASSI-EE is the first effort to leverage LLMs for automated code refactoring aimed at improving energy efficiency.

\section{Proposed LASSI-EE Automated Pipeline}\label{sec:lassiee}

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.90\textwidth]{figures/LASSI-EE_Pipeline.png}
    \caption{The LASSI-EE framework. The `Self-correct' components in Stages 1 and 3 are separate iterative feedback loops, with its schematic shown in Figure \ref{fig:thecorrectionpipeline}. %\lan{more caption needed to explain different shapes.}
    }
    \label{fig:thepipeline}
    \vspace{-1em}
\end{figure*} 

In this section, we describe LASSI-EE, a self-correcting framework that harnesses an LLM to refactor parallel codes for improved energy efficiency on a target compute platform and discuss its design in detail. Leveraging the capabilities of LASSI \cite{LASSI}, which incorporates domain knowledge, self-prompted summarizations, and self-correction feedback loops to resolve compilation and execution errors, LASSI-EE proposes novel strategies for the task of energy-efficient programming with an LLM. These include system-aware prompting, iterative refinement, LLM parameter tuning, self-prompted code reviews, an LLM-as-a-Judge agent for functional validation, and automated comparisons between the original and optimized code versions. Figure \ref{fig:thepipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline, highlighting the iterative stages of the framework. Given a parallel code and a parallel system as an input, LASSI-EE performs the performance and power profiling on the system (Stage 0), the baseline code refactoring using an LLM (Stage 1), further context-driven and self-prompted refactoring (Stages 2 and 3), and outputs the final energy efficient code (Stage 4). 
%Currently, LASSI-EE is implemented on GPUs and can be extended to other architectures. 
%the use of system information, self-prompted code reviews to identify inefficiencies and propose refactoring plans, targeted iterations on promising prior generations, LLM parameter tuning to explore a broader solution space, an LLM-as-a-Judge agent to assess the functional correctness of refactored code, and a final LLM-generated comparison between the original source code and the most improved refactored version.

%our extension follows a similar base framework that incorporates domain knowledge, self-prompted summarizations of background context and code, and self-correction loops to fix compilation and execution errors introduced into generated codes. LASSI-EE adds more features including system information, self-prompted code reviews to identify potential inefficiencies and propose a refactoring implementation plan, targeted iterations on prior generations that demonstrate improvement, LLM parameter tuning to help explore a broader solution space, an LLM-as-a-Judge agent to provide an evaluation of the functional validity of the refactored code, and a final LLM-generated comparison between the source code and most improved refactored code.

%Figure~\ref{fig:thepipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline, highlighting the iterative stages of LASSI-EE. Below, we outline the key characteristics of the pipeline, including prompting strategies, the integration of LLMs, and the multi-stage iterative processes used to generate refactored code that is more energy-efficient than the original source code. Currently, LASSI-EE is focused on GPUs, but can be extended to other architectures.

\subsection{Stage 0: Code Profiling}

% \lan{a paragraph to give an overview of stage 0}

The initial stage of the LASSI-EE pipeline captures the baseline performance metrics of an original parallel source code under consideration for energy efficiency on a given target system. This code is automatically compiled and executed, and its power profile is collected. These metrics are then stored in memory for later comparison.
%following the LLM-based generations of refactored code.

\subsubsection{LLM Agnostic}\label{sec:agnostic}
A key feature of LASSI-EE is bootstrapping the base performance of any LLM by extending its capability for a complex task through self-correction and iterative generation with feedback loops. As new private- and open-source LLMs are released, remaining agnostic to a particular class of LLMs (e.g., instruction-following or reasoning-based as well as model vendors) is essential for LASSI-EE to remain generalizable and scalable.  

LASSI-EE enables the implementation of any single LLM or, optionally, an \emph{ensemble} of LLMs, where a second, specialized LLM model can be leveraged for the code generation. Analyzing the sensitivity of various LLMs to the provided context and iterative feedback requires an understanding of the extent of possible increases in an LLM's base capability. The LASSI-EE framework provides an experimental implementation for such studies, which will be necessary as new models emerge that may alter the effectiveness of prompting strategies and agentic interactions within a generative pipeline, such as ours, for producing energy-efficient parallel codes.


\subsubsection{Power Profiling}\label{sec:powerprofile}

Essential to the energy efficiency task of LASSI-EE, the power profile of the code under consideration must be measured \emph{during} the pipeline operation. This measurement event first occurs in this initial stage when the original source code is compiled and executed. In particular, the power profile is captured during runtime, with the average power, the energy is derived from the power profile and runtime. Details on how these energy-related metrics are calculated and recorded are discussed in Section \ref{sec:results}.

The power measurements captured in Stage 0 provide a baseline to compare all subsequent refactored code generations and guide the decision-making of the pipeline for its next iterative steps. During Stage 1 and each iterative Stage 3 (Figure \ref{fig:thepipeline}), a similar power profile is captured immediately during the execution of the newly generated code. %that is successfully executed. 
These results are indexed and stored to track the pipeline's progress and identify which generated code provides the greatest reduction in energy consumption when the pipeline is terminated. 


\subsection{Stage 1: Baseline Code Refactoring}

% \lan{a paragraph to give an overview of stage 1}

After the source code profiling, the LASSI-EE pipeline starts Stage 1 by embracing the \emph{potential} base capability of the LLM to generate an energy-efficient variant of the source code. This initial attempt is passed through the same power profile and evaluation routine implemented later the pipeline. If this base LLM result offers the most energy-efficient refactoring, it will be identified as the final solution of the LASSI-EE process.

\subsubsection{Baseline Zero-Shot}

Without providing context or task-specific examples, this zero-shot prompting strategy \cite{BuildLLMs} instructs the LLM with a straightforward prompt, listed in Table \ref{tab:zeroshotprompt} for a C++ CUDA code, to refactor the source code. The pipeline does not leave the LLM entirely alone in this case, as the self-correction feedback module ("Self-correct" in Stage 1, Figure 1) (details shown in Figure \ref{fig:thecorrectionpipeline}) is triggered to compile and execute the zero-shot generated code, providing corrective feedback if needed to fix any bugs.

Upon compiling and executing the generated code from this initial try, a power profile is captured, the LLM-as-a-Judge agent (\S \ref{sec:llmjudge}) is called, and, if deemed valid, then a comparison is made between these energy metrics and those of the source code. If the zero-shot refactored code version is either invalid or does not demonstrate energy savings, then the source code is passed on to the next stage. However, if this first try implements measurable improvements, then the zero-shot version becomes the seed for subsequent code generations.

