\PromptText{\scriptsize You are an expert C++/CUDA engineer. Your goal is to refactor provided CUDA C++ code to improve energy efficiency by reducing GPU and CPU power consumption, while preserving all original functionality and expected output of the provided code. You must also carefully read and incorporate the best practices and CUDA optimization techniques from the provided background documents and refactoring plans. You always surround your generated code with the three characters \texttt{\textasciigrave\textasciigrave\textasciigrave}.}\end{tcolorbox}
\end{tabular}%
}
\label{tab:systemprompt}
\vspace{-3em}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Self-correcting}

Self-correction steps occur throughout the LASSI-EE pipeline. Immediately after each generation, the code is automatically compiled and executed to test its functionality and collect its power profile. If an error occurs as a result of either compilation or execution, then a self-correcting feedback routine is triggered that continues until all errors are eliminated. This novel component of LASSI-EE enables autonomous refactoring without the need for human intervention when a bug is introduced into the generated code.

As outlined in Figure \ref{fig:thecorrectionpipeline}, LASSI-EE attempts to rectify problematic code by capturing compiler errors and re-prompting the LLM with these details to generate a revised version. Following a successful compilation, the code proceeds to execution. Any runtime errors encountered are provided to the LLM with instructions and context (including the buggy code) to generate new code to resolve these issues. Cycling between compilation and execution persists until the generated code runs error-free. In Figure~\ref{fig:thepipeline}, this component is denoted as the `Self-correct' module. The correction prompt is listed in Table \ref{tab:correctionprompt}.

A maximum threshold of self-correction attempts is configurable in LASSI-EE to avoid a runaway pipeline. As a testament to the critical role this component plays, none of our trials reported in Section \ref{sec:results} terminated prematurely due to unrepairable code errors.

