\subsubsection{LLM-as-a-Judge Agent}\label{sec:llmjudge}

A fundamental challenge with \emph{automating} a code generation pipeline is verifying the functional validity of the resulting code. Whether the task is to refactor existing code or generate new code from scratch, the utility of the pipeline rests on knowing that the output code meets the expected requirements. 
%The previous work of LASSI \cite{LASSI} for translating code between languages also acknowledged this need, and employed a simple post-generation heuristic pairing the code successfully compiling and executing, along with a human review of the code's standard output to determine its pass of a basic validity test.

%While still not guaranteeing a generated code's correctness or functional equivalence to its corresponding source code, we attempt take code validation a step further to enable a more robust review that can be automated within the pipeline without human intervention. 
After each power profiling, we propose an LLM-as-a-Judge agent, prompted with the full original source code, the latest refactored code, and a strict instruction prompt. This judging agent is guided to compare the core functionality of the two code versions and assess whether they produce identical standard output—allowing for minor numerical differences that may result from energy-saving optimizations. The agent is instructed to return a final recommendation of either \texttt{VALID} or \texttt{INVALID}.
%In the step following each power profile measurement, we introduce an LLM-as-a-Judge agent that is prompted with the full original source code, the latest refactored code, and a strict prompt strategy. The judging agent is guided to compare expected core functionality of the codes and recognize identical standard output, other than numerical values that might be reasonably different resulting in any energy savings due to optimizations introduced during the refactoring. A final recommendation of \texttt{VALID} or \texttt{INVALID} is instructed to be provided by the judging agent. 
The pipeline extracts this result from the LLM's response to determine if this code is discarded or has its energy metrics compared with prior codes. Using the same system prompt as all other LLM calls throughout the pipeline, the instruction prompt for the LLM-as-a-Judge agent is provided in Table \ref{tab:judgemprompt}.


