\subsubsection{Context}\label{sec:context}
Instruction prompts for an LLM directly influence its response, which can be sensitive to specific details included in the instruction, even for energy efficiency coding tasks \cite{cappendijk_generating_2024}. We expand on this idea with the core prompting strategy of LASSI-EE being implemented through an enhanced context building phase. Occurring after the initial refactoring Stage 1 is complete, the pipeline curates LLM-authored summarizations of background knowledge and the incoming code (either the source or an optimized version from the zero-shot attempt) to prepare for developing a refactoring plan. The LLM is also guided to identify inefficiencies in the code and then proposes specific implementations to address these issues.

Background knowledge, referred to as the context, covering programming and best practices in C++ and CUDA optimization, along with the system state and hardware metrics, is provided to the LLM in standalone inference calls. Specifically, we source Chapter 2 in the CUDA C++ Programming Guide \cite{CudaProgramming} for background knowledge of the CUDA programming model and selected content from Chapters 12, 13, 14, and 15 in the CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide \cite{CudaGuide} for CUDA optimization recommendations.

In addition, the computing system running LASSI-EE is queried with command-line calls to extract detailed hardware configuration information, including GPU, CPU, DRAM, and power-related data. % each listed in Table \ref{tab:cli_commands}. 
For example, we use \textit{nvidia-smi} to retrieve NVIDIA GPU device details. The outputs from these system calls are labeled and provided to the LLM for summarization and later use within the pipeline.

As a one-time process, the LLM is individually prompted to generate summaries of background resources, system details, and the source code. These responses are then included as context for the subsequent refactoring plan generation described below in Section \ref{sec:planning}. By allowing the LLM to author such self-prompts instead of the full content of these original contexts, we expect that the LLM can more effectively process the most important details and recommendations from the official documentation as they pertain to its role specified through the system prompt. Table \ref{tab:selfcontext} listst he instruction prompts for generating these self-summaries.

