\subsection{Stage 3: Iterative Refactoring with Self-Prompting  and Self-Correction}
%\subsection{Iterative code refactoring}

%\lan{a paragraph to give an overview of stage 3. mention power profiling, self-correcting, and judge have similar logic as those in Stage 1.}
%\mtd{because there are no sub-sub sections here, I think this existing first paragraph provides an introduction}
%\lan{suggest to shorten this section to 1-2 paragraphs to describe the differences between stage 1 and stage 3.}
%\lan{it would be good to polish this subsection by highlighting how stage 3 differs from stage 1.  }
The heart of LASSI-EE is the Stage 3 implementation of the refactoring plan to instruct the LLM to generate a more energy-efficient parallel code. This plan differentiates the process here from Stage 1, where the LLM is prompted context-free with only basic instructions. The instruction prompt to perform the code generation in Stage 3 is listed in Table \ref{tab:generatecode}, which consistently results in the LLM responding with a brief description of its changes and a block containing the refactored code that can be extracted by the pipeline for evaluation. This generated code passes through the self-correction module (Figure \ref{fig:thecorrectionpipeline}) to test compilation and execution and iterate with feedback to eliminate code errors.  During the execution of the refactored code, the power profile is collected and stored, and the calculated average power and energy are captured if the execution completes successfully.

Next, the refactored code, its standard output, and energy metrics are handed off to the LLM-as-a-Judge (Section \ref{sec:llmjudge}) to recommend whether this revision is functionally valid. If not, then this version of the code is discarded, and Stage 3 begins again, applying the \emph{same} refactoring plan used previously. If the code passes the judging agent, then the energy metrics are compared to the last ``best'' code, which could be the source code, the zero-shot version, or any other refactored code generated during Stage 3. In this framework, We define ``best'' as the code version (including the source code) that compiles, executes, is marked \verb|VALID| by the LLM-as-a-Judge, and consumes the least energy. 

If the energy does not improve, then this code is still saved as a stored version, and Stage 3 restarts with the previous ``best code'' and refactoring plan. If more energy is saved compared to the previous best, then this revision is saved and sent back through to prompt the LLM to write a \emph{new} refactoring plan based on the latest optimizations.

During this Stage 3 iterative code generation, an adaptive adjustment is applied to the temperature parameter of the LLM when subsequent refactorings do not result in additional energy savings. The temperature is a dial that sharpens the probability distribution of the most likely next tokens to be included in the response. With the default LLM temperature set to a low 0.2 for LASSI-EE, the model's generations tend to be more deterministic. However, increasing this value will relax the probability distribution, allowing the model a broader selection of tokens and essentially making it appear more creative. Following no energy savings, the pipeline increments this value by 0.2 before iterating through another code generation attempt using the same refactoring plan. A stopping condition for the entire LASSI-EE pipeline is triggered after sufficient iterations occur such that the temperature value increment goes above 0.8. This scenario suggests that the pipeline has exhausted its capabilities to implement additional optimizations into the code revisions and that further iterations are unnecessary.

