\subsection{Experiment Configuration}

\subsubsection{LLMs.} 
While  LASSI-EE allows for an LLM ensemble, for this study, we selected a single LLM that was both readily accessible for our research, and among state of the art at the time these experiments were conducted. In our preliminary experimentation, we tested several of the latest models, including instruction-following and reasoning models. We also explored ensemble models, with one LLM for performing self-prompted summarizations and pre-generation evaluations, and another code-specialized LLM for exclusively generating the refactored code. We concluded that for the sack of simplicity, OpenAI's o3-mini as a standalone model for the entire pipeline demonstrated elevated capabilities over trials with other models, including Llama 3.3 \cite{Llama33}, QwQ-32B \cite{qwq32b}, Qwen2.5-Coder 32B \cite{hui2024qwen2}, DeepSeek-Coder-v2 \cite{deepseek-ai_deepseek-coder-v2_2024}, IBM's Granite Code 34B \cite{mishra_granite_2024}, OpenAI's GPT-4o, o1-preview, and o1-mini, and even open-source reasoning models, like DeepSeek-R1 70B \cite{deepseekr1}. However, the current o-series of reasoning models available from OpenAI at the time of this experiment, including o3-mini, do not support the \verb|temperature| tuning parameter \cite{AzureReasoningModels}, meaning that our experimental runs did not benefit from the automated temperature tuning. 

\subsubsection{Testbed.} 
Our experiments were conducted on a Chameleon testbed \cite{Chameleon}, featuring two AMD EPYC 7763 64-core processors (128 physical cores, 256 threads total) and a single NVIDIA A100 80 GB GPU. The system was running NVIDIA driver version 560.35.05 with CUDA 12.6. The LASSI-EE code base is developed in Python 3.12.3 for this study. Interfacing between LASSI-EE and the system command line was facilitated through the \verb|subprocess| library, specifically for the calls to get system state information and compile and execute code.  

