    nvcc -std=c++14 -Xcompiler -Wall -arch=sm_80 -O3
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Power Measurement.}
%During this initial test of the source code and each subsequent test of refactored codes, 
The power profile is collected during runtime, and the average power and energy are calculated. Compared with the baseline measurements from the source code, subsequently generated codes can be determined automatically by the pipeline if energy savings are realized in the refactored version. 

In this study, we focus on C++ CUDA codes for GPUs 
%GPU workloads—applications 
which primarily rely on GPUs rather than CPU cores for computation. Accordingly, our power measurements focus on the GPU's power consumption. The pipeline utilizes \verb|pyNVML|, the Python bindings to the NVIDIA Management Library, to collect instantaneous power measurements from the GPU. Before executing the code under test, the NVML library is initialized to prepare for communication with the NVIDIA driver and hardware.

%\lan{commented off your paragraphs for saving space. check the following two paragraphs.} 
Power usage is sampled every 0.01 seconds, beginning with a brief initial period to estimate average idle power. During code execution, power is collected in a separate thread at the same interval. Timestamps are recorded at the launch and completion of code execution to mark the runtime window in the power profile and energy calculations. The collection continues for 15 seconds post-execution to capture a stable post-run idle state. These pre- and post-run idle values are averaged to estimate idle system power during execution.

To ensure accurate comparisons, both within a single pipeline run and across multiple runs, we subtract idle power from all measurements to calculate \emph{net power usage}. This correction accounts for idle power drift, especially during longer sessions. \emph{Net energy} is then estimated by summing $N$ idle-subtracted power samples $P_i$ multiplied by its time interval ($\Delta t = 0.01 s$), approximating the continuous energy integral, such that
\vspace{-0.5em}
\begin{equation}
E_{net} \approx \Delta t \sum_{i=1}^{N} \max\{P_i, 0\}
\end{equation}
Any negative power measurements are clamped to zero to avoid non-physical outcomes due to noise or the subtraction of the idle power level, which could lead to negative values.

\begin{comment}
Power usage is collected at 0.01 s intervals for a a few seconds to establish a initial idle state by calculating an average idle power. Then, a separate thread is created to continue collecting power during runtime, again at 0.01 s intervals. A timestamp is collected at the moment the code execution command is launched as well as soon as the return code from the execution is received designating the runtime is complete. These times are used to mark the beginning and end of code execution in our power profile plots and energy calculations. The power collection continues for an additional 15 seconds after runtime, sufficient to allow the GPU to settle and then capture another idle power state. From these before and after idle states, we estimate an average idle power representing the background state of the system during runtime. 

When \emph{comparing} average power and energy consumption of each executed code within a single pipeline run and across multiple runs, we found it necessary to correct for the idle state in our reported measurements. Within a single pipeline process of iterative code executions, some of which could last in excess of 20 minutes, the idle power could drift causing direct comparisons between subsequent codes to misrepresent the net effects of any introduced optimizations.
Therefore, in this work, we use \emph{net power usage} which is defined as power consumption by subtracting idle power.
%Therefore, as seen in the exemplar power profile in Figure \ref{fig:powerprofile} measured when executing the \verb|jacobi| source code, the power values have idle power subtracted, which extends into our average power and net energy consumed during runtime. 

Our calculation of energy approximated the integral of the instantaneous power during the runtime. Specifically, as we captured GPU power measurements at fixed 0.01 s intervals, we estimate the total energy consumption by employing a Riemann sum approach. Only data points within the window of our recorded runtime start and end times. Therefore, each idle-substracted power measurement $P_i$ can be multiplied by the corresponding time interval (approximately 0.01 s), and their sum yields an approximation of the continuous energy integral, such that


\begin{equation}
E \approx \Delta t \sum_{i=1}^{N} \max\{P_i, 0\}
\end{equation}
Here, $N$ represents the number of power measurements during execution, \(\Delta t\) is the time interval between consecutive power measurements, and $E$ is the derived net energy consumed, expressed in joules. To avoid non-physical outcomes due to noise or the subtraction of the idle power level, which could lead to negative values, any negative power measurements are clamped to zero.
\end{comment}

\subsection{Experimental Results}

As expected, responses from an LLM are not strictly deterministic. Therefore, we applied the LASSI-EE automated pipeline to the 20 source codes listed in Table \ref{tab:sourcecode} and repeated each run three times.
Across the three trials for each source code, several runs resulted in similar energy savings in the final refactored code. However, many others varied significantly between runs, ranging from negligible energy savings or functionally invalid solutions to double-digit percentage improvements over the original source code.
%Within our three trials for each source code, we observed several resulting in similar energy savings in the final refactored code. However, many others varied between runs from little to no energy savings or functionally invalid solutions to double-digit percentage savings from the source code. In the next section, we discuss some of these outlier solutions and lessons learned that inform future improvements for this approach to a code generation framework. 
We report our results in Table \ref{tab:results}, showing one of the three trials for each tested source code. The selected results represent either the best outcome or a representative example from these trials. 
%Using our full configuration details presented here and our source code that is publicly available, a deeper analysis on the consistency of the pipeline across repeated trials will offer an interesting exploration into the nature and controllability of specific LLMs implemented with LASSI-EE. 

Across all experimental trials, including the three runs for each of the 20 source codes, LASSI-EE generated a more energy-efficient refactored code compared to its source code in 73.3\% of total trials (85\% of source codes). Of these, an average of 47.36\% energy savings was realized, along with an average 31.34\% reduction in the average power and an average 51.93\% faster runtimes. Also, within these energy-saving refactored codes, 56.8\% showed improvements in both average power \emph{and} runtime, 11.41\% in only average power, and 31.8\% in only runtime.

The span of measured energy savings was broad across all trials, from a modest 2.35\% in \verb|floydwarshall| to 99.56\% in \verb|bsearch|, as seen in Table \ref{tab:results}. We also see ranges in the reduced average power from 1.96\% to 93.04\% and faster runtimes from 1.72\% to 93.68\%. While these reported ``best'' codes from the pipeline each passed the \verb|VALID| check by the LLM-as-a-Judge agent, suggesting that the refactored code remained functionally equivalent to their source, as described in Section \ref{sec:llmjudge}, we consider some of these outlier cases with massive reductions in the next section.

