\section{LASSI-EE Insights and Extensions}\label{sec:discuss}

%\lan{this section can be expanded to include (1) lessons learned from the work, (2) strategies suggested by LLms for saving energy and errors made, and (3) limitations of this word: only test with o3mini, limited test cases in terms of number of apps and only A100, etc.}

We highlight several key observations from our case study with the LASSI-EE framework, including insights and extensions that can guide future enhancements to improve automated code generation that supports energy-efficient parallel programming.

\textbf{Limits of Base LLMs in Energy-Aware Programming---}
%Base LLM capability is still not enough
As our hypothesis for this study is that the base capability of an LLM can be augmented through a framework such as LASSI-EE, we observe this anticipated outcome through a few trials of the zero-shot refactoring attempt. We see in Table \ref{tab:results} that lists the top trials for each code, the base LLM, when coupled with the self-correcting feedback loop, generated one time the best code refactoring, denoted as Code Version 0, for the entire pipeline run. In this case, the pipeline generated six additional attempts (the ``Refactor Count'') during Stage 3, but none of these revisions provided more energy savings. Over all trials (three separate runs for each of the 20 source codes), the zero-shot result was the best in only three runs.

On the other hand, across all our trials, the base LLM generated a zero-shot response for the refactored code that was flagged as \verb|INVALID| by the LLM-as-a-Judge agent 25\% of the time. This counting excludes those of \verb|marchingCubes|, from which \emph{no} code generated from the entire pipeline was flagged as functionally valid compared to the source code. In all other trials, even if not the most energy-efficient revision, at least one generated code from the Stage 3 process of LASSI-EE provided a \verb|VALID| solution. 

Therefore, our approach of embracing the base capability of an LLM when it performs well \emph{combined} with the context-infused, iterative, and self-prompted refactorings offers a strong strategy toward consistent energy-efficient parallel code generation.


\textbf{LASSI-EE's Diverse Refactoring Strategies--- }
%Refactoring strategies}
The LASSI-EE pipeline introduced a variety of optimization strategies in its refactored versions of the application source codes that demonstrated improved energy efficiency. The LLM-based code comparison stage of the pipeline assisted in identifying around 50 specific techniques present in the refactored codes but not in the original source. 

At a high level, we categorize these optimization strategies and count how many codes implemented at least one technique from each category, as summarized in Figure \ref{fig:strategies}: (1) Memory \& data management, (2) algorithm \& instruction optimization, (3) communication \& synchronization, (4) kernel execution, (5) cache \& memory access, and (6) thread cooperation. For example, many refactorings included implementing pinned (page-locked) memory, which is known for enabling efficient data transfer between the host and device \cite{CUDApinnedmem}. Other frequent optimization techniques seen in the refactored codes include implementing CUDA streams, using the cached and read-only constant memory space for fixed values, precomputation of values, and using CUDA events for timing.

