%\subsection{Outlier solutions}
\textbf{Outliers: Need for Human-in-the-Loop Validation--- } Several refactored codes presented dramatic energy savings, exceeding $90\%$ compared to the source code, as listed in Table \ref{tab:results}. 
Although the LLM-as-a-Judge agent flagged all these codes (excluding those marked as N/A) as functionally valid, such significant improvements may still warrant skepticism.
Incorporating some level of human-in-the-loop review alongside AI processes remains essential to increase confidence in such results. 
Here, we look closer at two refactored code examples, 
\verb|threadfence| and \verb|bsearch|. The former represents an effective refactoring case, and the latter illustrates a misleading or false positive result.

In the original source code of \verb|threadfence|, the input array is copied from the host to the device at the start of every iteration of the repeat loop, as shown in Table \ref{tab:threadfence_compare}(a). In contrast, the refactored code moves this \verb|cudaMemcpy()| to a one-time operation before the loop initiates (Table \ref{tab:threadfence_compare} (b)). To isolate the impact of this change, we manually modified the refactored code by placing the memory copy back inside the loop in the refactored code. As a result, energy consumption increased significantly—from 55.82 J (Table 10) to 273.06 J, indicating a substantial 79.6\% improvement in energy savings just due to the optimized placement of the memory copy.


While LASSI-EE identifies several refactoring strategies for energy efficiency, it can sometimes apply misleading ones—such as removing repeated computations. The source code of \verb|bsearch| includes an input parameter of \verb|repeat| to replicate the calculations based on user configuration. This parameter remains in the refactored version of the code, but the loop is nowhere to be found, as seen in the code comparison in Table \ref{tab:bsearch_compare}. 
The LLM-as-a-Judge agent detects the loop's removal but lacks sufficient prompt guidance to flag the refactoring as \verb|INVALID|. With further tuning, the agent could be improved to handle such cases.

%Several optimization strategies are identified from the LLM-based code comparison during Stage 4 of LASSI-EE, including using constant memory for precomputed values, enhanced memory access with \verb|__ldg()|, pinned (page-locked) host memory and asynchronous transfers instead of \verb|posix_memalign| for host memory allocation, and dedicated CUDA streams and synchronization. This comparison also calls out a culprit: removing repeated computations and unneeded. The source code intentionally includes an input parameter of \verb|repeat| to replicate the calculations based on user configuration. This parameter remains in the refactored version of the code, but the loop is no where to be found, as seen in the code comparison in Table \ref{tab:bsearch_compare}. 


\begin{comment}
In this scenario, the LLM-as-a-Judge agent also picks up on the elimination of the \verb|repeat| loop. However, the guidance provided int the prompting to the agent is not sufficient to lead it toward an invalid determination. With further experimentation, adjusting the behavior of the agent to handle such a scenario should be possible.

If we look through the other generated codes in the \verb|bsearch| pipeline run reported in Table \ref{tab:results}, we find that code version 1 also resulted in energy savings. Without introducing suspect strategies, this revision consumes 2,345.63 J with an average power of 230.87 W and a runtime of 10.17 s, corresponding to less dramatic but notable improvements of 7.17\%, 1.78\%, and 5.48\%, respectively. We do not update our results in Table \ref{tab:results} with this alternative code version, but we do acknowledge that LASSI-EE did produce a more performant code, despite the LLM-as-a-Judge agent failing to recognize the suspect optimization. 
