\textbf{Cross-Language and Cross-Architecture Extensions--- }
First, as described in \S \ref{sec:agnostic}, LASSI-EE is designed to be LLM-agnostic, making it well-suited for extension to other LLMs or ensembles of LLMs.
Second, while all experiments were conducted in a single compute environment (i.e., an NVIDIA A100 GPU using CUDA C++), 
the LASSI-EE framework is designed to \emph{be readily extensible} to other architectures, programming models such as openMP and SYCL, and devices. 
By modifying the system prompt and incorporating language-specific syntax, semantics, and optimization heuristics, LASSI-EE could support code refactoring for other multi-core processors or emerging AI accelerators. 
Additionally, incorporating hardware-aware profiling and extending the feedback and validation modules to support multiple backends would enable broader applicability, positioning LASSI-EE as a general-purpose tool for energy-efficient parallel code transformation across many computing platforms.

%This study has several limitations. First, we restricted our evaluation to OpenAI’s o3-mini model, which, at the time of data collection, demonstrated the best zero-shot performance among the models we tested, including GPT-4o and several code-focused open-source models such as Qwen2.5-Coder 32B. Second, all experiments were conducted in a single compute environment, an NVIDIA A100 GPU using CUDA C++, with fixed compiler and driver configurations. While this setup simplified the analysis, the LASSI-EE framework is designed to be readily extensible to other architectures, programming models, and devices. Finally, we limited our dataset to 20 codes from the HeCBench suite, selecting only those with minimal external dependencies. Expanding to more complex benchmarks could provide deeper insights but may require further customization of the pipeline to support more involved build and execution workflows.

\textbf{Agentic AI for Energy-Aware Programming--- }
LASSI-EE represents a compelling step toward the vision of Agentic AI and AI co-scientists \cite{aicoscientist, xie_toward_2023}, which offers a framework that generates, debates, and evolves hypotheses to support scientific discovery. Similarly, LASSI-EE employs a multi-stage, self-correcting pipeline to iteratively refactor parallel scientific applications for energy efficiency. 
By integrating components like the LLM-as-a-Judge agent, self-prompting context, and feedback-driven self-correction loop, LASSI-EE demonstrates how LLMs can autonomously reason about, modify, and evaluate complex scientific code, exhibiting agentic behavior. %\lan{need polish here}

\begin{comment}
Several limitations were intentionally imposed in our experimental design to reduce complexity and focus on a case study of how the LASSI-EE pipeline can enhance LLM-driven generation of performant parallel codes. However, these offer useful targets for future study that could lead to further improvements to the LASSI-EE framework. 

As described in Section \ref{sec:llmselected}, we report results only from tests using OpenAI's o3-mini model as the core LLM for the entire pipeline. New models were less readily available at the time data collection began. Heuristically, we found this model to be performing the best at the zero-shot attempts compared to many other models tested, including GPT-4o and several code-specific open-source models, like Qwen2.5-Coder 32B. We wanted to ensure our current case study could leverage the most capable base LLM, so we decided to not expand the study to compare over a range of LLMs, as we suspected overall performance would drop. That said, we also expect that the zero-shot attempts within LASSI-EE may improve when using more recent models that have been released around the time of this writing. However, the framework would should elevate their capability to a higher level in this specific task. 

We also limited the case study to a single compute environment and parallel programming language. The LASSI-EE framework is easily extensible to other systems and languages. Although the current version targets a single NVIDA A100 GPU and CUDA C++ with fixed background context, compiler commands, and power measurement driver bindings, additional system scenarios can be added with documented best practices for other languages, corresponding compilation frameworks, and drivers to interface with other accelerator devices. 

Finally, we limit our data collection to 20 codes from the HeCBench suite. More codes are available from this benchmark, but many do not fit the requirements we set for this study of having no more than one external file dependency. Other parallel code collections can be explored, such as the Altis Benchmark Suite \cite{Altis}, which offers more complex codes with deeper dependencies. However, additional customization of LASSI-EE for automatically compiling and executing the code may be required due to less straightforward code build processes for these benchmarks.  
\end{comment}

\section{Conclusion}

As energy demands strain both infrastructure and the environment, pursuing energy efficiency across the HPC stack is increasingly critical. However, most LLM-based code generation efforts prioritize functional correctness over performance and energy efficiency. In this study, we presented LASSI-EE, an automated, LLM-based refactoring framework designed to generate energy-efficient parallel code tailored to the target execution platform. Using a multi-stage, iterative process, LASSI-EE achieved an average energy reduction of 47\% across 85\% of the 20 HeCBench benchmarks evaluated on the NVIDIA A100 GPU system. Our work highlights the potential of augmenting LLMs to generate correct code and enable energy-aware programming. Additionally, we identify key insights, challenges, and limitations of the current framework, offering direction for future enhancements.

We will release LASSI-EE, along with the associated datasets, as \textbf{open-source on GitHub} to support reproducibility and foster collaboration within the HPC and AI communities.



