\begin{lemma}
\label{edge-lemma}
    Any guard $g\in\Gamma^*$ that is in the interior of $P$  lies on exactly one edge-extension. Moreover, the corresponding edge belongs to $\partial(g)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    First, observe that $g$ cannot be on two edge-extensions because otherwise $g$ is in $Q$, a contradiction.
    If $g$ is not on any edge-extension in $\partial(g)$ as in Figure~\ref{reflex-boundary-fig}, then by moving $g$ along the bisector of $w(g)$ one can increase $w(g)$ without decreasing its coverage $\partial(g)$, a contradiction to our choice of $\Gamma^*$---this movement is possible as $g$ is in the interior of $P$ and $w(g)<2\pi$ by Lemma~\ref{2pi-lemma}. Thus $g$ lies on the extension of exactly one edge, say $e$. If $e\notin \partial(g)$ then we can get a similar contradiction by slightly moving $g$ towards the bisector of $w(g)$.
\end{proof}

