\section{Introduction}
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We consider the optimal control problem for a controlled stochastic differential equation (SDE)
of the form
\begin{equation} \label{eq:FSDE}
{\rm d}X_t = b(X_t){\rm d}t + G(X_t) U_t {\rm d}t + \Sigma^{1/2} {\rm d}B_t, \qquad X_0 = a,
\end{equation}
under either the finite horizon cost function
\begin{equation} \label{eq:costJ}
J_T(a,U_{0:T}) = \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_0^T \left(c(X_t) + \frac{1}{2} U_t^{\rm T} R^{-1} U_t \right) {\rm d}t + f(X_T) \right]
\end{equation}
or the infinite horizon discounted cost function
\begin{equation} \label{eq:costJ infinite}
    J(a,U_{0:\infty}) = \mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^\infty e^{-\gamma t}\left(c(X_t)+ \frac{1}{2} U_t^{\rm T} R^{-1} U_t\right){\rm d}t \right].
\end{equation}
Here $B_t$ denotes $d_x$-dimensional Brownian motion, $\Sigma \in \mathbb{R}^{d_x\times d_x}$ the symmetric positive definite diffusion matrix, $R \in \mathbb{R}^{d_u\times d_u}$ a symmetric positive definite weight matrix, $G(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_x\times d_u}$ the possibly position-dependent control matrix, $c(x)\ge 0$ the running cost, $f(x)\ge0$ the terminal cost, and $\gamma >0$ the discount factor. See, for example, \cite{Carmona,Meyn} for more details. We also introduce the weighted norm $\| \cdot \|_R$ via
\begin{equation} \label{eq:norm}
\|u\|^2_R = u^{\rm T} R^{-1} u.
\end{equation}
The aim is to find the closed loop control law $U_t = u_t(X_t)$ that minimizes the cost (\ref{eq:costJ}) or (\ref{eq:costJ infinite}), respectively, over the set of admissible control laws.

It is well known, that the associated optimal control problems for ordinary differential equations (ODEs); i.e.~$\Sigma = 0$ in (\ref{eq:FSDE}), can be solved by the Pontryagin minimum 
(maximum) principle (PMP) \cite{pontryagin}. The PMP has been extended to SDEs (\ref{eq:FSDE}) in terms of forward-backward SDEs \cite{Peng1993,Carmona,Bensoussan}. In this paper, we provide an alternative extension via a deterministic mean-field formulation, which leads to interacting particle implementations in the form of Hamiltonian ODEs. The formulation is attractive from a computational perspective since it allows for a decoupling of the forward and backward evolution equations for finite horizon optimal control problems and a forward simulation for infinite horizon optimal control problems. The proposed mean-field approach also delivers closed loop control laws in contrast to the classical PMP for ODEs \cite{pontryagin}. The proposed methodology can then also be used to approximate viscosity solutions for controlled ODEs by setting $\Sigma = \epsilon I$ for sufficiently small $\epsilon$ \cite{CL83}.

Mean-field formulations and their interacting particle approximations have been considered before in the context of the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) equation \cite{Carmona,Bensoussan}. The key idea is to connect the time evolution of the value function to a probability density function evolving under an appropriate nonlinear Fokker--Planck equation and its mean-field representation \cite{MO22,JTMM22,reich23,Prashant24}. In this paper, we directly approximate the gradient of the value function and derive appropriate Hamiltonian evolution equations; thus extending mean-field formulations to the PMP. We denote our mean-field formulation the McKean--Pontryagin minimum principle.

The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. The following Section \ref{sec:math} summarizes key mathematical results for finite horizon optimal control problems at the level of the underlying HJB and Fokker--Planck equations \cite{Carmona,Pavliotis2016}. The novel mean-field formulation of the PMP is provided in Section \ref{sec:Hamiltonian} starting from an Eulerian variational perspective in terms of the HJB equation. Following \cite{Salmon88,holmI}, the arising action functional is subsequently transformed into a Lagrangian formulation with trajectories following mean-field Hamiltonian ODEs. The infinite horizon optimal control problem is treated in Section \ref{sec:discounted}. Here the key result is a pair of forward mean-field ODEs, which asymptotically deliver the desired closed loop control law. A number of numerical approximation techniques are discussed in Section \ref{sec:implementation}; including an approximation based on discrete Schr\"odinger bridges \cite{MarshallCoifman,WR20} as outlined in Section \ref{sec:DM}. The McKean--Pontryagin formulation is tested for two  infinite horizon discounted cost (\ref{eq:costJ infinite}) optimal control problems in Section \ref{sec:experiments}. The first example is provided by a controlled inverted pendulum \cite{Meyn} while the second example is provided by a controlled Lorenz-63 system \cite{KK24}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
\section{Mathematical background} \label{sec:math}
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

It is well-known \cite{CL83,Carmona} that, assuming sufficient regularity, the desired  closed loop control law is provided by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:optimal control law}
u_t(x) = - R G(x)^{\rm T} \nabla_x v_t(x)
\end{equation}
with the optimal value function $v_t(x)$ satisfying the HJB equation
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:HJB}
-\partial_t v_t = b \cdot \nabla_x v_t + \frac{1}{2}  \Sigma : 
D_x^2 v_t + c -  \frac{1}{2} \|RG(x)^{\rm T}\nabla_x v_t\|^2_R, \qquad v_T = f.
\end{equation}
Here $A:B = \sum_{i,j} a_{ij}b_{ij}$ \cite{Pavliotis2016}. In case of deterministic dynamics; i.e.~$\Sigma = 0$ in (\ref{eq:FSDE}), the PMP provides the open loop optimal control $U_t = -RG(X_t)^{\rm T}P_t$ along the solution $X_t$ of (\ref{eq:FSDE}) by solving the ODEs 
\begin{subequations} \label{eq:deterministic PMP}
\begin{align}
    \dot{X}_t &= b(X_t) - G(X_t)R G(X_t)^{\rm T} P_t,\\
    -\dot{P}_t &= (D_x b(X_t))^{\rm T}P_t - \frac{1}{2} 
    \nabla_x\|R G^{\rm T}(X_t) P_t\|_R^2 + \nabla_x c(X_t)
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
in $(X_t,P_t)$ subject to the boundary conditions $X_0 = a$ and $P_T = \nabla_x f(X_T)$ \cite{pontryagin}. Formulation (\ref{eq:deterministic PMP}) offers significant computational savings compared to (\ref{eq:HJB}) and has been widely used especially in the context of nonlinear model predictive control (NMPC) \cite{Allgower,MPC}. The PMP for ODEs has been extended to SDEs (\ref{eq:FSDE}) giving rise to the, so called, stochastic PMP with (\ref{eq:deterministic PMP}) replaced by a pair of forward-backward SDEs
\cite{Carmona,Bensoussan}.

In this paper, we develop a Hamiltonian mean-field formulation in the spirit of the PMP-based Hamiltonian ODE formulation (\ref{eq:deterministic PMP}), which, in turn, can be approximated by interacting particle systems. Since the PMP directly targets the gradient of the value function $v_t(x)$; i.e.,~$P_t = y_t(X_t)$ with $y_t(x) = \nabla_x v_t(x)$, we note that 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Pontryagin}
    -\partial_t y_t = \nabla_x (y_t \cdot b) +
    \frac{1}{2} \nabla_x (\Sigma : D_x y_t) + \nabla_x c - \frac{1}{2}\nabla_x \|RG^{\rm T}y_t\|^2_R, \quad
    y_T = \nabla_x f,
\end{equation}
which follows form (\ref{eq:HJB}) assuming sufficient regularity of the value function. 

Substituting the optimal control law (\ref{eq:optimal control law}) into (\ref{eq:FSDE}), the law $\pi_t$ of the states $X_t$ satisfies the Fokker--Plank equation
\begin{equation} \label{eq:FPE}
\partial \pi_t = - \nabla_x \cdot \left(\pi_t (b + Gu_t) \right) + \frac{1}{2}
\nabla_x \cdot (\Sigma \nabla_x \pi_t), \qquad \pi_0 = p_0,
\end{equation}
for given initial density $p_0$ \cite{Pavliotis2016}.

We assume throughout this paper that the diffusion matrix $\Sigma$ is position independent. However, the proposed PMP extends to position dependent $\Sigma(x)$. We also allow for $\Sigma =0$, in which case our formulation provides the optimal closed loop control law instead of the open loop solution provided by (\ref{eq:deterministic PMP}). In this case, adding diffusion via $\Sigma = \epsilon I$ with $\epsilon \to 0$ sufficiently small allows one to numerically approximate viscosity solutions of (\ref{eq:HJB}) \cite{CL83}.

