\paragraph*{Adding probabilistic choice}

We next consider two %
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
probabilistic
extensions (binary and countable):
\begin{align}\label{eq:add_prob}
  %
                                       M,N \Coloneqq  &\;
                                                        \inpu{k}(M_i)_{i
                                                       \in\arity{k}}
                                                        \mid M+_p N  \\
 %
  M,N \Coloneqq & \; \inpu{k}(M_i)_{i\in\arity{k}}\mid
                  \sum_{n\in\nats} p_n\ml M_n
\end{align}
The first extension provides programs of the form \mbox{$M+_p N$}, where $p$
ranges over the interval $(0,1)$.  Such a program chooses either 0 or 1 with
probability $p$ and $1-p$ respectively, then executes $M$ or $N$
accordingly.

The second extension provides programs of the form ${\sum_{n\in\nats}
p_n\ml M_n}$, where $(p_n)_{n\in\nats}$ is a sequence of nonnegative
reals that sum to 1.  Such a program chooses $n\in\nats$ with
probability $p_n$, then executes $M_n$.

These calculi are the probabilistic counterparts of nondeterministic
calculi studied in~\cite{BowlerLevyEtAl18}.  

%
%
%
%
%
