Grassroots Federation aims to address \textbf{the egalitarian formation and the fair democratic governance of large-scale, decentralized, sovereign digital communities}.  As such, it may offer the first concrete proposal to realize \emph{global democracy} (aka \emph{cosmopolitan democracy})~\cite{archibugi2004cosmopolitan} since envisioned more than two centuries ago by Kant~\cite{kant1795perpetual}.  After all, social networks have shown that a single digital platform can bring billions of people together; the remaining challenge is to build one that does so  democratically.  Developing the conceptual and mathematical foundations for doing that is the subject of this paper

%
In this introduction we discuss the aim; the challenges to achieving it; and how the approach presented here may overcome these challenges.


\subsubsection{Aim.}

We elaborate on the terms in the statement of the aim:
%
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Egalitarian formation:} The digital counterpart of freedom of assembly~\cite{mill1859liberty}---the ability of any group of people, armed with networked personal computing devices (aka smartphones), to assemble into a sovereign digital community. 

    \item \textbf{Sovereign:} Not at the behest of, or under the digital control of, any third party such as Zuckerberg, Musk, Miners, Validators, etc. See Figure \ref{figure:economist}.

    \item \textbf{Fair democratic governance:} Equitable participation of the population of the digital community in  the democratic process.  This also entails the inability of any outside third-party to digitally control or subvert the democratic process---another facet of digital sovereignty.  
    %
    If the community is a federation of several child communities, then fair representation of the populations of the child communities in the democratic governance of the parent community.

    \item \textbf{Large-scale:} The size of the EU, the US, existing social networks, and even humanity at large.

    \item \textbf{Decentralized:} Without any central control. In particular, the ability of multiple such digital communities to form independently of each other and potentially subsequently to federate.  (The formal term for distributed systems and protocols having this property is \emph{grassroots}~\cite{shapiro2023grassrootsBA,shapiro2025atomic}).

    \item \textbf{Sovereign digital communities:} As alluded to above, communities of people, armed with smartphones, that can form and self-govern digitally, not at the behest of, or under the digital control of, any third party.\footnote{Smartphones can best realize this ability when provided with a universal and neutral network access; but in its absence it can also be realized in principle using peer-to-peer smartphone ad-hoc networking (SPANs)~\cite{lacava2022securing}.}
\end{enumerate}


\subsubsection{Challenges.}

Achieving the aim faces three key challenges:
%
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Digital Voting:} Large-scale digital voting is considered untenable~\cite{park2021going}.
    \item \textbf{Digital Identity:} Existing large-scale digital communities harbor multitudes of \emph{sybils}---fake and duplicate digital identities---that hamper fair democratic governance, and in particular violate ``one person -- one vote''~\cite{facebook2024sybils}.
    \item \textbf{Digital Platform:} Members of existing global digital platforms---centralized and decentralized alike---are not their sovereign.  Hence such platforms are not commensurate with digital freedom of assembly and do not offer a suitable foundation for the egalitarian formation of sovereign  digital communities~\cite{shapiro2022foundations,shapiro2024grassroots,shapiro2025atomic}.
\end{enumerate} 


\subsubsection{Addressing the challenges.}

A grassroots federation evolves via the grassroots formation and federation of sovereign digital communities.
Small communities (say up to $100$ members) govern themselves; larger communities---no matter how large---are governed by a small assembly elected by sortition among its members. The approach supports digital freedom of assembly---allowing people to freely form  digital communities that can federate into ever-larger communities as well as spawn child communities,  based on geography, jurisdiction, affiliations, relations, interests,  causes, and more.  
We contend that Grassroots Federation provides conceptual and mathematical foundations for addressing the three challenges presented above, as outlined next.

\begin{enumerate}

\item
\textbf{Supplant large-scale digital voting by grassroots sortition.}
%
In Grassroots Federation any large community---no matter how large and how rich in internal structure---is governed democratically by a small (e.g., $\approx 100$) assembly, selected by sortition from its population. 
Thus, such a small assembly can employ small-scale democratic processes, notably deliberation, consensus building, and voting by show of hands.   Work on equality in the democratic governance of digital communities, including
equality in  constitution formation and amendment~\cite{abramowitz2021beginning}, in voting and proposing in elections, legislation, and budgeting~\cite{bulteau2021aggregation}, in deliberative coalition formation~\cite{elkind2021united}, and in community forking~\cite{abramowitz2021democratic}, is directly applicable to democratic governance by a digital assembly.  

However, employing digital sortition instead of large-scale digital voting also poses a challenge.
In a \emph{given population} with a \emph{given strata} (e.g. female/male, rural/urban, young/old), sortition can ensure that the assembly represents the strata perfectly, in expectation~\cite{FGGP20,EKMP+22,Bour13,FKP21,flanigan2021fair}.  However, the two \emph{given} in the previous sentence presume a \emph{giver}---an authority that determines both membership in the population and the strata that must be represented. How can this authority be elected democratically?

Grassroots Federation resolves this circularity by the people themselves determining the populations from which assemblies are selected and the strata to be represented:  If people freely assemble and assemblies federate democratically, they thus establish the populations and the strata for sortition independently of any external authority.  

\item
\textbf{Sybil-repellence and sybil-resilience in the formation and democratic governance of digital communities.}
%
Existing large-scale digital communities suffer from extensive penetration of sybils.  For example,  Facebook removes around 1Bn fake accounts every quarter~\cite{facebook2024sybils}.
Most work on sybil detection and elimination employs top-down approaches suitable for global platforms~\cite{tran2009sybil,douceur2002sybil,seuken2014sybil,cheng2005sybilproof,RN230,RN363}, but by their nature do not provide support for the democratic formation and conduct of digital communities.  Egalitarian  sybil-repelling protocols for the grassroots formation of digital communities were developed~\cite{poupko2021building,shahaf2020genuine}, based on mutual trust among the people forming a community and their willingness to backup this trust with mutual sureties.  These protocols ensure, under certain conditions, that a digital community can grow indefinitely while retaining only a bounded fraction of sybils.

While these protocols were designed for a single digital community, the approach can be generalized also to community federation:  Two communities federate only if they know, trust  and can verify each other to be sybil-repelling (possibly verifying that they use the same sybil-repellence protocol, or performing due-diligence on each other's protocols). Socially, such knowledge and trust can be attained through having a sufficient number of people in both communities knowing and trusting people in the other community or, better yet, for the communities to have a sufficiently-large intersection---people who are members of both communities and are known and trusted by other members in both communities. (Indeed, making the above social claim a mathematical claim is a natural future work.)

A complementary line of work~\cite{shahaf2019sybil,meir2024safe} shows how a community can employ sybil-resilient democratic governance:  How the safety and liveness of digital democratic processes can be ensured despite a bounded penetration of sybils, even in the face of partial participation by its non-sybil members.
Together, the two lines of work offer a comprehensive solution for (\ia) sybil-repellence during the egalitarian formation of digital communities and (\ib) sybil-resilience in the democratic governance of digital communities and their federation, the two processes that are the backbone of Grassroots Federation.

\item
\textbf{Computational foundation for grassroots platforms in general and Grassroots Federation in particular.}
%
The Internet is dominated by centralized global platforms---social networks, Internet commerce, ‘sharing-economy’---with autocratic control~\cite{zuboff2019age,zuboff2022surveillance}.  An alternative is emerging --- blockchains and cryptocurrencies~\cite{ethereum:dao,faqir2021comparative,bitcoin,ethereum,wang2018overview,wang2021ethereum}--- that are also global platforms, but with decentralized, plutocratic control~\cite{vitalikplutocracy}, and hence are not a suitable foundation for egalitarian, sovereign, democratic digital communities.


Grassroots platforms~\cite{shapiro2023grassrootsBA,shapiro2022foundations,shapiro2024grassroots,shapiro2024gc,shapiro2025atomic} aim to offer an egalitarian alternative to global platforms, centralized and decentralized alike.  Global platforms can only have a single instance---one Facebook, one Bitcoin---as two instances of the platform would clash over global resources such as domain name/port number/boot nodes, which are hardwired into their code.  It is possible to modify their code (`hard-fork') to create non-conflicting instances---Facebook$'$, Bitcoin$'$---which would ignore, rather than interoperate with, the primary instance. 

Grassroots platforms, in contrast, can have multiple instances that emerge and operate independently of each other and of any global resource except the network, and can interoperate and coalesce once interconnected, forming ever-larger platform instances, potentially (but not necessarily) coalescing into a single global instance.   Any platform that operates on a shared global resource or employs a global replicated (Blockchain~\cite{bitcoin}), or distributed (IPFS~\cite{benet2014ipfs}, DHT~\cite{rhea2005opendht}) shared data structure, or distributed pub/sub systems with a global directory~\cite{chockler2007constructing,chockler2007spidercast,buchegger2009peerson}, are all not grassroots. Server-based federated systems such as Mastodon~\cite{raman2019challenges} are also not grassroots, informally due to the control exerted by each server-operator on its members; formally, since a group of people of any size cannot function without first connecting to such a server.

\end{enumerate}


\subsubsection{The Grassroots Aspect.}

Grassroots Federation is an example of a \textit{grassroots platform} (as opposed to a \emph{global platform}, centralized or decentralized). A simpler, `direct-democracy' instance of Grassroots Federation, which captures the grassroots formation of communities and their federation, but abstracts-away the selection of assemblies, was formally defined and proven grassroots in previous work~\cite{shapiro2025atomic}.  In that specification, a decision of a community to join or leave another is realized by an atomic transaction in which all members of both communities participate, as opposed to just the members of the two assemblies.  Therefore the previous work serves as an abstraction of direct digital democracy, rather than of democratic governance by assemblies selected by sortition, as presented here.  It thus would require very large-scale consensus and digital voting, which may be untenable.

Here, the Grassroots Federation protocol is more involved, as it also includes the selection, rotation and maintenance of assemblies in a dynamic setting.  While the dynamics of federation is similar in both works---federations form and communities join or leave---the focus of this work is on the fairness of representation and equity of participation in assemblies selected, rotated and maintained by sortition. It is a follow-on on previous work~\cite{halpern2024federated} that examined this very question, but in the static setting of a given federation structure.

We expect the proof in~\cite{shapiro2025atomic}, showing that the `direct democracy' variant of Grassroots Federation is indeed grassroots to carry over to the `people's assembly' variant presented here, but leave this proof to future work. Here, we focus on the fairness of selection by sortition of assemblies by Grassroots Federation.   In particular, fair representation of child communities in the assembly of the parent community and equitable representation of the population of a community in its assembly.


\subsubsection{Paper Structure.}

The rest of the paper is organized as follows.
Section \ref{section:dynamic} introduces Dynamic Federation Graphs, which are the abstract data type encoding the structure of a grassroots federation.  It then presents an abstract transition system $\calF$ that specifies how a grassroots federation can evolve over time.
Section  \ref{section:fairness} recalls the fairness requirements and measures employed in earlier work on the static setting---how to populate the assemblies of a given federation graph~\cite{halpern2024federated}.  It then adapts these requirements and measures to the dynamic setting considered here, of an evolving grassroots federation.
Section~\ref{section:fair-TS} integrates the fairness requirements to define a fair federation transition system; it describes $\calF\calF$, a restriction of the federation transition system $\calF$, and proves it to be fair.
%
Section~\ref{section:pragmatics} considers extending $\calF\calF$ with upward mobility, 
Section~\ref{section:outlook} discusses future directions, and Section~\ref{section:epilogue} provides an Epilogue on global digital democracy.  The rest of the paper is mathematical and quite formal; the reader not interested in such may skip to the Epilogue.


\section{Dynamic Federation Graphs}\label{section:dynamic}

We describe what is a valid federation (essentially a graph representing the federation structure and recording the assembly of each community, containing the representatives of each of the child communities);  we then describe what are valid federation protocols (essentially protocols whose states are always valid federations)---these serve as components of our mathematical foundation.


\subsection{Federation Graph}

We assume a set of \emph{agents}, or \emph{people}, $\Pi$.  While $\Pi$ is potentially infinite (think of all the people yet to be born), we only consider finite subsets of it, so in the following $P\subset \Pi$ always refers to a finite set of people $P$.

In a directed graph $G$, the \emph{children} of a node $v\in G$, $\children_G(v)$, is the set of nodes pointed to by edges in $G$ that emanate from $v$, $\children_{G}(v) := \{ u : v\rightarrow u 
\in G\}$, with $G$ omitted if clear from the context.  Also,  $v$ is a \emph{parent} of each of its children and is a \emph{leaf} if it has no children.

\begin{definition}[Federation, Community, Representative (Tied and Floating), Assembly (Member), Population]
A \temph{federation} is a labeled directed acyclic graph $G=(V,E)$ where each node $v\in V$ is referred to as \temph{community}. Each community $v\in V$ is labeled by an \temph{assembly}, denoted by $A_v$, which is finite set of pairs $(p,u_p)$, each referred to as a \temph{representative},  where $p\in \Pi$ is a person and $u_p\in \children(v) \cup \{\bot\}$. If $u_p =\bot$, then the representative is \temph{floating}, else  \temph{tied} to $u_p$, with  $A_v(u) := \{(p,u) \in A_v: p\in \Pi\}$.
%
The \temph{assembly members} of $v$ are $M_v:= \{p: (p,v_p)\in A_v\}$ and the 
\temph{population} of $v$, $P_v$, is the union of the assembly members of the leaves reachable from $v$.
Let $\calG$ denote the set of all finite federations.
\end{definition}
For $G=(V,E)$ we sometimes use $v\in G$ and $e\in G$ instead of $v\in V$ and $e \in E$.
In the following, we assume a fixed $n>0$, which is the target size of assemblies; for concreteness, imagine $n \approx 100$.  It may be possible in the future to have $n$ be a function of the size of the community or even to relativize $n$ per community while still upholding our results (see discussion in Section~\ref{section:outlook}).

We are interested in federations in which communities are  \emph{valid}, as defined next.
\begin{definition}[Valid Federation] 
Let $G\in \calG$.
A community $v\in G$ is \temph{valid} in $G$ if the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Unique members:} If $v$ is a leaf, then $A_v= \{(p,\bot)\}$ for some $p\in \Pi$ and $A_v \ne A_{u}$ for any other leaf $u\ne v$ in $G$.
    \item \textbf{Unique representation:} If $v$ is not a leaf, then for every $(p,v_p) \in A_v$, $v_p \in \children(v)\cup \{\bot\}$; and if $(p,v_p), (p,v'_p) \in A_v$ then $v_p =  v'_p$.
    \item \textbf{Representation by the population:} If $|P_v|\le n$ then $M_v = P_v$, else $|M_v|=n$ and  $M_v\subset P_v$.
    \item \textbf{Recursive validity:} Every $u\in \children(v)$ is valid in $G$.   
\end{enumerate}
%
A federation $G\in \calG$ is \temph{valid} if every community $v\in G$ is valid in $G$.
\end{definition}



\begin{remark}
%
Note that a valid federation need not be connected:  It may consist of several disconnected valid federations. 
%Note the requirement of $P_u \geq P_v / n$ -- this is needed for the mathematics but not limiting dramatically, and is quite standard in representative bodies.
%
\end{remark}

