Given the definition of a valid federation, we can consider its dynamics; we start with a rather general definition of a timed transition system.

\begin{definition}[Timed Transition System]
A \temph{timed transition system} $TS=(S,s_0,T)$ consists of a set of \temph{states} $S$ with a designated \temph{initial state} $s_0\in S$ and \temph{timed transitions} $T\subset S\times \mathbb{N} \times S$ s.t  $s\ne s'$ for any $s\xrightarrow{t} s' \in T$.  A state $s\in S$ is \temph{final} if $s\xrightarrow{t} s' \notin S$ for any $t\ge 0, s'\in S$.
A \temph{run} of $TS$ consists of a sequence of states $s_0\xrightarrow{t_1} s_1 \xrightarrow{t_2} s_2 \ldots $  s.t. $s_i\xrightarrow{t_{i+1}}s_{i+1} \in T$ for all $i\ge 0$, which is finite only if its last state is final.  Given such a run $r$ and $k, t \ge 0$, then the \temph{time of the $k^{th}$ state of $r$} is $t(k) := \sum_{i\le k} t_i$, and 
$s(t)$, the \temph{state at time $t$}, is defined to be the maximal state $s_j$ such that $t(j)  ~\le~ t$.
\end{definition}
When there is no potential confusion we use $s_t$ as a synonym for $s(t)$.
\begin{remark}
%
Essentially, a timed transition system is like an event-based system where the state is constant between transitions; or, alternatively, it is a transition system between states where each transition takes some time.
%
\end{remark}

Next, we consider the dynamics of a transition system over valid federations. In particular, the federation transition system considered below abstracts away any communication, deliberation, and negotiation details within or among communities, and only expresses their outcomes: A parent community is formed, one community becomes a member of another or ceases to be a member of another, and a person becomes an assembly member or ends their term as a member.



%\nimrod{Udi -- we also need to add the term limits to the formal model, somehow. (Again, if we move to a unit-clocktick model, I think that we can say something like "in each timestep, consider a label, consider someone in this label, then it must not be in the label for some later $t' < t + "year"$ or something of this sort.. I'm not sure what's the most elegant way to plug this into the formal model.}\udi{OK}\nimrod{So I leave this for now for you; we can discuss of course..}

Next we define the federation transition system and prove that its runs produce only valid federations.  We often use $u, v\in V$ for child communities and $f\in V$ for a parent community.



\begin{definition}[Federation Transition System]
A \temph{federation transition system} is a timed transition system $\calF =(\calG,G_0,T)$ with federations $\calG$ as states, the empty federation $G_0=(\emptyset,\emptyset)$ as an initial state, and transitions $T$ that include all transitions $G\xrightarrow{t} G'$, $i\le i'$, $t\ge 0$, for any valid federation $G\in \calG$ and any $G'\ne G$ produced from $G=(V,E)$  in one of the following ways: 
%
\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Emerge $p\in \Pi$}:  Add a node labeled $\{(p,\bot)\}$ to $G$ provided $G$ has no such node.

\item \textbf{Federate $v\in G$:} Add a new node $f$ to $V$, set $A_f:= A_v$ and add the edge  $f\rightarrow v$ to $E$.

    \item \textbf{Join $f\rightarrow v$:} Add $f\rightarrow v$ to $E$ provided that $v, f\in V$ and  $G'$ is acyclic.


    \item  \label{leave-transition} \textbf{Leave $f \rightarrow v$:}  If  $f\rightarrow v \in E$ then remove  $f\rightarrow v$ from $E$ and remove $A_f(u)$ from $A_f$.

    \item \label{remove-transition}\textbf{Remove $(p,v_p)$ from Assembly $f$:} If $(p,v_p)\in A_f$, then remove it.

    \item\label{add-transition} \textbf{Add $(p,v_p)$ to Assembly of $f$:} If $|A_f|<n$ and $M_f\subset P_f$ then add $(p,v_p)$ to $A_f$ for some $p\in P_f\setminus M_f$ and some $v_p\in \children(f)\cup \{\bot\}$.

    \item  \label{gc-transition} \textbf{Garbage Collect:} If $A_v=\emptyset$ for a leaf $v\in G$ then remove $v$ from $V$, and for every edge $f\rightarrow v \in E$, remove this edge from $E$ and remove $A_f(v)$ from $A_f$.
\end{enumerate}
Transitions \ref{add-transition} and \ref{gc-transition} take priority in that other transitions can be taken only if neither of them are enabled,  and they take zero time $t=0$.

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
%
Note that a state in which  transitions \ref{add-transition} or \ref{gc-transition} are enabled is not valid.
Hence, the last condition in the definition ensures that there is no time $t\ge 0$ such that $G_t$ has transitions \ref{add-transition} or \ref{gc-transition} enabled. In other words, any transition that enables transitions \ref{add-transition} or \ref{gc-transition}, together with the application of these transitions to completion, are carried out atomically, in zero time.  
%
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}
In every run of $\calF$, $G_t$ is valid for every $t\ge 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We prove this by induction on the length of the run. Let $G_0$ be the initial state. By definition, $G_0 = (\emptyset, \emptyset)$, is a valid federation.

Assume that $G_t$ is valid at time $t$. We show that after applying a transition $G_t \xrightarrow{t'} G_{t'}$, the resulting federation $G_{t'}$ is also valid.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Emerge $p \in \Pi$:} A new leaf node labeled with $\{(p, \bot)\}$ is added. This satisfies the validity condition that relates to leaves, namely the uniqueness of persons across leaves, since no other node has the same singleton label. Validity of existing nodes is unaffected.

    \item \textbf{Federate $v$:} A new node $f$ is added with $A_f := A_v$ and edge $f \rightarrow v$. Since $A_v$ was valid, $A_f$ is a subset of $P_f = P_v$, which is valid. The rest of the federation remains unchanged. Recursive validity holds since $v$ was valid.

    \item \textbf{Join $f \rightarrow v$:} An edge is added to the graph if it remains acyclic. Validity is preserved because the structure remains a DAG and no assembly is modified directly.

    \item \textbf{Leave $f \rightarrow v$:} The edge $f \rightarrow v$ is removed, and representatives in $A_f$ tied to $v$ are also removed. This maintains the unique representation invariant (including that only persons in the population can be in the assembly) and the graph remains a DAG.

    \item \textbf{Remove $(p, v_p)$ from $A_f$:} A representative is removed from an assembly. Since transitions \ref{add-transition} and \ref{gc-transition} are applied to completion (atomically), validity is preserved by ensuring that assemblies either include all of $P_f$ or are a size-$n$ subset of it.

    \item \textbf{Add $(p, v_p)$ to $A_f$:} This occurs only if $|M_f| < |P_f|$ and $|A_f| < n$, and only $p \in P_f \setminus M_f$ is added. Hence, validity is maintained. Since these additions are done to completion before time progresses, intermediate invalid states are never exposed.

    \item \textbf{Garbage Collect:} A leaf node with an empty assembly is removed, and its parent edges and assembly references are cleaned up. Since a leaf with an empty assembly violates validity, this action restores validity.
\end{enumerate}

Finally, due to the restrictions inherent in each of the possible transitions, and since transitions \ref{add-transition} and \ref{gc-transition} are applied atomically in zero time and take precedence, we never observe an intermediate state where they are partially applied. Hence, at every observable time $t$, $G_t$ is valid.
%
\qed\end{proof}

So, the federation transition system described above is valid in the sense that any reachable federation is valid. 


\section{Fair Representation and Equitable Participation}\label{section:fairness}

Next, we consider fairness properties for the federation graphs that are reached in the federation transition system defined above.
%
We first discuss previous work, dealing with a static federation; and then present properties that can be satisfied in the dynamic setting.

\subsection{Fairness in the Static Setting}

Previous work~\cite{halpern2024federated} considered a fixed federation and asked how can its assemblies be populated in a way that respects natural ex~post and ex~ante constraints on the representation of child communities within a parent assembly, as well as on the fair selection of individual members of a community to its assembly.  The ex~post constraints ensured that the actual representation of a child community in the assembly of the parent community would be at least its equal share, rounded down to the nearest seat.  The ex~ante constraint ensured that the expected representation of a child community in the assembly of the parent community would be equal to its share, and that the probability of all members of a community to be selected to the assembly of a community is the same.  To address these constraints,  selection probabilities were  judicially assigned to individuals as the assemblies were populated. An additional constraint that was undertaken was upward-mobility, discussed in Section~\ref{section:pragmatics}. The selection process was proven to succeed while respecting all these constraints for some federation structures, but not in general.

\begin{remark}
%
In the static case (and thus also in the dynamic case studied here) one major challenge is the integrality constraint -- that assembly members are complete agents that cannot be split. In this context, it is reminiscent of problems that relate to, e.g., apportionment methods~\cite{apportionment}.
%
\end{remark}


\subsection{Fairness in the Dynamic Setting}

In the dynamic setting the challenge seems bigger, but so are the degrees of freedom to address it: On the one hand, the ex~post constraint has to be satisfied not only once, but at all times.  On the other hand, any unfairness in representation or selection can be corrected over time.  Thus,  the ex~ante constraint on  probabilities of a one-shot selection process for a fixed federation is replaced by a constraint on the actual representations in a sequence of federations being fair in the limit, or the actual participation in a sequence of federations being fair in the limit, so that any bias may have till infinity to be corrected.

This may seem an artificial construction, as communities, let alone people, do not survive till infinity to enjoy such promised fairness or equity.  However, this approach is standard in computer science due to its merits.  For example, a prevailing model in distributed computing is that of \emph{eventual synchrony} (aka \emph{partial synchrony}), which assumes that eventually---in some indefinite time in the future, but before infinity---the system will become synchronous.  
Such a time is referred to as Global Stabilization Time (GST).
The practical value of the model is that indeed real systems are often synchronous, and even if not they often soon become such,  and when they do the proposed protocols work well.  
% NIMROD simulations: Furthermore, analyses and simulations are carried out to show how quickly does the protocol work well as soon as the system becomes synchronous.

Similarly here, formally speaking, fair representation and equitable participation are required to be attained only in the limit, and only after the federation structure stabilizes, if ever.  However, any specific protocol satisfying this requirement can then be analyzed as to how quickly does convergence occur in practical scenarios in which the federation structure stabilizes for a long-enough period.

\mypara{Term limits}
%
Regarding term limits, for simplicity we assume that the constitution of the federation specifies that assembly members are selected for a fixed term $\Delta$ (say $\Delta= 1$ year, or any other term), unless they are removed sooner due to changes in the federation structure, in particular the leaving of a child community of which the assembly member represents.  However, a uniform term limit may not be necessary for our results, so it is also possible that the constitution would allow each community to decide on their own term limit and even modify it.  Here, we assume a fixed term limit for all.



Next we discuss requirements for the fairness of representation for a dynamic federation, following previous work~\cite{halpern2024federated}. As the structure of a federation may change arbitrarily during a run of a federation transition system, a natural thing to do is look at a specific community $f$ that emerges during the run and present two types of representation requirements on $f$:
Properties that correspond to the ex~post requirements of~\cite{halpern2024federated} holding not only once but  all times.  And properties that correspond to the ex~ante requirements of~\cite{halpern2024federated} holding not once in expectation but in actuality in the limit, provided the federation structure eventually stabilizes.


\subsection{Representation and Participation Measures}

Here we first introduce notation for representation and participation measures and then use it to define static and dynamic measures.

\mypara{Notation} We employ several measures for representation of the population of a community within a parent community's assembly, and for the participation of a member of a community in the community's assembly, as follows, all with a subscript $t$ to denote a particular time.  We follow the weighting scheme of~\cite{halpern2024federated}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Relative Weight:} $\omega1$, a real number between $0$ and $1$. 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\omega1(p,v,f)$  measures the relative weight of a member $p$ of $f$ within its child community $v$. %\nimrod{so why do we need $v$ in the definition? If it is a member of $f$ then it is a member of some child $v$ of it (unless $f$ is the singleton), no?} \udi{I have changed it to be a characteristic function, so $v$ is needed. See rest of definition.}
        It is 1 divided by the number of child communities of $f$ that $p$ is a member of, if $p$ is a member of $v$, zero otherwise.
        That is, the \emph{relative weight of a person} $p\in P_f$ in $v$ is: 
    $$
    \omega1(p,v,f)  := 
    \begin{cases}
			\frac{1}{|\{{u ~:~ p \in P_{u} \wedge u \in \children(f)\}|}} & \text{if $p\in P_v$}\\ \\
            0 & \text{otherwise}
		 \end{cases}
    $$ 
        \item $\omega1(v,f)$ measures the relative weight of a child community $v$ within a parent community $f$, which is the sum of the relative weights of the members of $f$ within $v$.
        That is, the \emph{relative weight of a child community} $v$ in $f$ is:
    $$\omega1(v,f) := \sum_{p\in P_v} \omega1(p,v,f)$$
    \end{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{Proportional Weight:}  $\omega(v,f)$, a real between 0 and $n$.  It is the relative weight $\omega1(v,f)$ multiplied by the size of the assembly $n$, giving the number of representatives (possibly including a fractional representative) $v$ deserves in $f$ according to the relative weight $\omega1(v,f)$. 
    %\nimrod{Can we add this then: "So, formally, $\omega(p, f) = n \cdot \omega1(p, f) / |P_f|$; and $\omega(v, f) = n \cdot \omega(v, f) / |P_f|$?}\udi{We can, but I thought of introducing notation conceptually here and formally later.  If you want to do it once, then we also need to define $\omega1$ formally above.}\nimrod{understood}

    \item \textbf{Actual Representation:} $\sigma$, an integer between $0$ and $n$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\sigma(p,f)$, 1 if $p$ is a member of the assembly of $f$, 0 otherwise.
        \item $\sigma(v,f)$, the number of members of the assembly of $f$ that are members of the population of $v$ (whether floating or tied to $v$).
    \end{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{Mean over time:} For $\omega$ and $\sigma$, we measure their mean over time,  denote them by  $\bar\omega$ and $\bar\sigma$, respectively, and use them to specify the eventual fairness requirements.  \emph{Eventually-fair representation} requires that, in the limit, the mean actual representation $\bar\sigma$ of a child community within a parent community is at least the mean proportional weight  $\bar\omega$ of the child community within the parent community.  \emph{Eventually-fair participation} requires that, in the limit, the mean participation $\bar\sigma$ of a member of a community in its assembly is at least $n$ divided by the size of the community.
\end{enumerate}


\mypara{Static Measures} 
Note the following observation.

\begin{observation}\label{observation:one}
The weight function $\omega1$ satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Given $p\in P_f$, let $\textsc{c}(p,f) := \{v\in\children(f) : p\in P_v\}$, then 
    $\sum_{v\in \textsc{c}(p,f)} \omega1(p,v,f) = 1$.
    \item Given $f$, $\sum_{v\in \children(f)} \omega1(v,f)=|P_f|$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{observation}

% \udi{We need a similar one but in the limit...}

\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Fix $p \in P_f$. Let $\textsc{c}(p,f) = \{v \in \children(f) : p \in P_v\}$. By definition of $\omega1$, for each such $v$, we have $\omega1(p,v,f) = \nicefrac{1}{|\textsc{c}(p,f)|}$
    and $\omega1(p,v,f) = 0$ for $v \notin \textsc{c}(p,f)$. Therefore,
    \[
    \sum_{v \in \textsc{c}(p,f)} \omega1(p,v,f) = |\textsc{c}(p,f)| \cdot \frac{1}{|\textsc{c}(p,f)|} = 1.
    \]

    \item Fix $f$. By definition,
    \[
    \sum_{v \in \children(f)} \omega1(v,f) = \sum_{v \in \children(f)} \sum_{p \in P_v} \omega1(p,v,f).
    \]
    By exchanging the order of summation:
    \[
    = \sum_{p \in P_f} \sum_{v \in \textsc{c}(p,f)} \omega1(p,v,f).
    \]
    Using part (1), we know that for each $p \in P_f$, the inner sum is 1. Therefore:
    \[
    \sum_{v \in \children(f)} \omega1(v,f) = \sum_{p \in P_f} 1 = |P_f|.
    \]
\end{enumerate}

This finishes the proof.
%
\qed\end{proof}

The \emph{proportional weight} $\omega$ in a parent community $f$ is the relative weight $\omega1$ translated to its weighted proportional share in the parent assembly $A_f$ (informally speaking, it is the fractional number of seats in the assembly of $f$ that $p$ or $v$ deserve): 
%\nimrod{and this is no longer needed, right?}\udi{No, we use $\omega$ extensively, what do you mean `not needed'? Before it was $\omega^*$, but I renamed $\omega\rightarrow \omega1$ and $\omega^* \rightarrow \omega$.}
$$
\omega(v,f) := \frac{n}{|P_f|} \cdot \omega1(v,f)
$$
$$
\omega(p,v,f) := \frac{n}{|P_f|} \cdot\omega1(p,v,f)
$$


For a given run and a given time $t$, we use $A_{t,f}$, $M_{t,f}$, and $P_{t,f}$ to denote $A_f$, $M_{f}$, and $P_f$ in $G_t$, respectively, and similarly for $\omega1_t$ and $\omega_t$.  In addition, for a person $p\in P$ and community $f$, define:
$$
\sigma_t(p,f) := 
 \begin{cases}
			1 & \text{if $p\in M_{t,f}$}\\ 
             0 & \text{otherwise}
		 \end{cases}
$$ 
For communities $v$ and $f$, define:
$$\sigma_t(v,f) := |M_{t,f} \cap P_{t,v}|$$ 
or, equivalently:
$$\sigma_t(v,f) := \sum_{p\in P_{t,v}} \sigma_t(p,f)$$ 
denoting the number of members of $P_v$ in $M_f$ at time $t$.
If $t$ is understood from the context (usually understood as ``now''), then it is sometimes omitted. 


\mypara{Dynamic Measures} 
To state the eventual representation and participation requirements we consider their behavior in the limit. For a function $X(t) : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ from time to the reals we use
$\lim_{t \to \infty}X(t)$ as usual:  It is defined if there is a constant $c$ such that for every $\epsilon>0$ there is a time $t'\ge 0$ such that for every $t\ge t'$, $|X(t)-c|\le \epsilon$, and in
such a case $\lim_{t \to \infty}X(t)=c$. 

Theoretically, we are interested in the convergence of the functions defined above.  In practice, the question is how fast does each function converges, namely for any given~$\epsilon$, what is the minimal (or expected) time $t$ that satisfies the convergence condition.  For now, we just consider the theoretical question---whether the function converges, and if so to what value. The speed of convergence, to be analyzed via computer-based simulations, is deferred to future work.

For convenience, we define the mean of $X(t)$ from $0$ to $t'$ as usual:
$$
\bar X(t') := \frac{\int_{0}^{t'}  X(t) ~ dt}{t'}
$$


Given a run $r$, a community $f$ that occurs in $r$, and either a person $x\in P$ or a child community $x$ of $f$ that occurs in $r$, we define $\bar\sigma_t(x,f)$, which measures the mean participation/representation ratio  $x$ in the assembly of community $f$, by:
$$
\bar \sigma_{t'}(x,f) := \frac{\int_{0}^{t'}  \sigma_t(x,f) ~ dt}{t'}
$$
And, for a child community $v$ of $f$ that occurs in $r$, we measure its mean proportional weight in the assembly of $f$ by: 
$$
\bar \omega_{t'}(v,f) := \frac{\int_{0}^{t'}  \omega_t(v,f) ~ dt}{t'}
$$
Or, equivalently:
$$
\bar\omega_t(v,f) := \frac{n}{|P_f|}  \cdot\overline{\omega1}_t(v,f)
$$

\begin{remark}
Indeed, we are interested in the limit of the mean: It is not the case that the existence of $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma$ implies the existence of  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \sigma$, as the former might hold even thought the latter fluctuates by, e.g., $+1$/$-1$ till infinity.  This is typical when a child community has a fractional seat, and is always the case for individual participation.    
\end{remark}


\subsection{Persistently-Fair Representation}


\begin{definition}[Persistently-Fair  Representation]\label{definition:persistent-fair-representation}
Let $r$ be a run of a federation transition system over $P$ and $f$ a community valid in $r$.  Then $r$
satisfies \temph{persistently-fair representation} for $f$ if, for every time $t\ge 0$ and every $v\in G_t$, if $v \in \children(f)$ at $G_t$, then: 
$$
\sigma_t(v,f) \ge \floor{\omega_t(v,f)}
$$
\end{definition}

That is, we wish that each child community persistently receives a representation that is the floor of its proportional weight in the parent community's assembly.

\begin{example}
%
For example, assume $n=3$ and consider a community with two unequal child communities: community A has 3 members, while community B has 2 members. The goal is to ensure that each community gets its proportional share of representation in the parent community’s 3-seat assembly. Without taking the floor, exact proportional representation would allocate 1.8 seats to Federation A and 1.2 seats to community B, which is not possible since the number of seats must be an integer---hence the requirement with the floor function (rounding down) allocates at least 1 seat to community A and at least 1 to community B (thus, either $1$ for $A$ and $2$ for $B$ or vice versa), ensuring a reasonably-fair yet implementable representation.  
%
\end{example}


\subsection{Eventually-Fair Representation}

While we take the floor for the persistently-fair representation, eventually we wish to guarantee exact fair representation; this is our first eventual property---the eventually-fair representation requirement.
%
With this notion we can define eventually-fair representation for child communities in the assembly of the parent community. 

\begin{definition}[Eventually-Fair Representation]\label{definition:eventuallly-fair-representation}
Given a run $r$ of a federation transition system and a community $f$ valid in $r$, we say that $f$ satisfies  \temph{eventually-fair representation} in $r$ if for every $v$ such that $v\in \children(f)$ holds for a suffix of $r$ and the following limits exist, the following holds:
$$
\lim_{t \to \infty}\bar\sigma_t(v,f)\ge \lim_{t \to \infty}\bar\omega_t(v,f)
$$
\end{definition}

Note that we use $\geq$ (and not $=$) since assembly members that are each a member of the populations of several child communities contribute to the representation of all these child communities, possibly resulting in some child communities being over-represented without others being under-represented.


\subsection{Eventually-Equitable Participation}



\begin{definition}[Eventually-Equitable Participation]\label{definition:eventuallly-fair-participation}
Given a run $r$ of a federation transition system over $P$ and a community $f$ valid in $r$,
we say that $f$ satisfies  \temph{eventually-equitable participation} in $r$ if for every $p\in P_f$ for a suffix of $r$:
%
    $$\lim_{t \to \infty}\bar\sigma_t(p,f) = \min \{\lim_{t \to \infty}\frac{n}{|P_{t,f}|}, 1\}$$
\end{definition}

Note that if the limit does not exist then the equality does not hold. Also,
if the community size is eventually less than $n$, then all community members are also members of its assembly, namely the mean participation ratio is $1$, but not more.  The definition
requires equality and not $\ge$ since, unlike in child communities where overlap of the underlying populations is possible, if one person over-participates in the limit then necessarily another one under-participates in the limit.


\section{A Fair Federation Transition System}\label{section:fair-TS}


\subsection{Fairness Requirements of a Federation Transition System}

We combine the three notions above (one persistent, two eventual) and apply them to all communities that occur in a run to obtain the general notion of a fair run.  
\begin{definition}[Fair Run]
Given a valid federation transition system $TS$,
a run $r$ of $TS$ is \temph{fair} if it satisfies persistently-fair representation (Definition \ref{definition:persistent-fair-representation}), eventually-fair representation (Definitions \ref{definition:eventuallly-fair-representation}), and eventually-equitable participation (Definition \ref{definition:eventuallly-fair-participation}) for every community $f$ in a suffix of $r$.
\end{definition}

We then add the notion of an eventually-stable run, in which the structure of the federation eventually stabilizes (even though assembly members keep rotating), and refer to the time at which this happens as
\emph{Federation Stabilization Time} (\emph{FST}), inspired by the eventual-synchrony notion of GST.  We define a fair federation transition system to be one in which eventually-stable runs are fair.

\begin{definition}[Eventually-Stable Run, Fair Federation Transition System]
Given a valid federation transition system $TS$,
a run $r$ of  $TS$  is \temph{eventually stable} if there is a \temph{Federation Stabilization Time (FST)} $t^*\ge 0$ such that for every $t>t^*$, $G_t$ and $G_{t^*}$ are identical up to non-leaf labeling. The transition  system $TS$ is \temph{fair} if every eventually-stable run of it is fair.
\end{definition}

\nimrodtemp{
