The following Theorem greatly simplifies proving our subsequent fairness claims.
\begin{theorem}[Equitable participation implies fair representation, eventually]\label{theorem:equitable-implies-fair}
If, in an eventually-stable run of a valid federation transition system a community satisfies eventually-equitable participation, then it satisfies eventually-fair representation.
\end{theorem}

To prove the Theorem we employ the following two Lemmas:

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:sigma-sum-of-limits}
For an eventually-stable run $r$ of a valid federation transition system over~$P$ with FST $t^*\ge 0$, and
a child $v$ of $f$ in $G_{t^*}$, it holds that:
$$
\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(v,f) =   \sum_{p\in P_{t^*,v}} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(p,f)
$$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $P_v := P_{t^*,v}$.  By their definitions (and as we are considering a time after GST in which  the set $P_v$ does not change), for $t\ge t^*$,
$$
 \sigma_t(v,f) = \sum_{p\in P_{v}}  \sigma_t(p,f)
$$
Hence,
%\nimrod{not always swapping summation and limit is fine; I wonder how to justify that here it is fine?}\udi{It is true if the limits exists, and after FST they exist.}\nimrod{So this is a general result of calculus, that "if limit exists then we can change order"?}\udi{Yes, algebraic limit theorem}
\[
\sum_{p\in P_v} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(p,f) =
 \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \sum_{p\in P_v} \bar\sigma_t(p,f) =
 \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \sum_{p\in P_v}  \frac{\int_{0}^{t} \sigma_{t'}(p,f) dt'}{t} =
\]
\[
\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}  \frac{\int_{0}^{t} \sum_{p\in P_v}\sigma_{t'}(p,f) dt'}{t} =
\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}  \frac{\int_{0}^{t} \sigma_{t'}(v,f) dt'}{t} =
\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(v,f)\ .
\]
The equalities hold if all limits exist, which they do due to the assumption of the eventual stabilization of the structure of the federation.
\qed\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:omega-sum-of-limits}
For an eventually-stable run $r$ of a valid federation transition system over $P$ with FST $t^*\ge 0$ and
a child $v$ of $f$ in $G_{t^*}$, it holds that:
$$
\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\omega_t(v,f) =   \sum_{p\in P_{t^*,v}} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\omega_t(p,v,f)\ ;
$$
and the same holds for $\overline{\omega1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Similar to the proof of Lemma \ref{lemma:sigma-sum-of-limits}, since under similar assumptions and by definition, for $t\ge t^*$:
$$
 \omega_t(v,f) = \sum_{p\in P_{t^*,v}}  \omega_t(p,v,f)\ .
$$
This finishes the proof of the lemma.
%
\qed\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[of Theorem \ref{theorem:equitable-implies-fair}]
By way of contradiction, assume that $r$ is an eventually-stable run of a valid federation transition system over $P$ with FST $t^*\ge 0$, and that there is a community  $f\in G_{t^*}$ that satisfies eventually-equitable participation but not eventually-fair representation in $r$. If  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} |P_{t,f}|\le n$, then both conditions are trivially satisfied, so assume $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} |P_{t,f}| > n$.  Thus, $f$ has a child $v$ in $G_{t^*}$ for which: 
$$
(1)~~ \lim_{t \to \infty}\bar\sigma_t(v,f) < \lim_{t \to \infty}\bar\omega_t(v,f)
$$
Let $P_v := P_{t^*,v}$. By applying Lemmas \ref{lemma:sigma-sum-of-limits} and \ref{lemma:omega-sum-of-limits} to inequality $(1)$ we obtain:
\[
(2)~~  \sum_{p\in P_{v}} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(p,f)   <  \sum_{p\in P_{v}} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\omega_t(p,v,f)
\]
Since both summations in inequality $(2)$ are over the same index set $P_v$, this implies that for at least one $p\in P_v$ we must have the following inequality:
\[
(3)~~  \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(p,f)   <   \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\omega_t(p,v,f)
\]
which can be extended by:
\[
(4)~~  \le \lim_{t \to \infty} \sum_{u\in \children(f)} \bar\omega_t(p,u,f) =  \lim_{t \to \infty} \bar\omega_t(p,f) =  \lim_{t \to \infty}\frac{n}{|P_{t,f}|}
\]
and summarized with:
\[
(5)~~  \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \bar\sigma_t(p,f)   <   \lim_{t \to \infty}\frac{n}{|P_{t,f}|}
\]
which contradicts eventually-equitable participation of $p$ in $f$.
\qed\end{proof}


\subsection{A Would-Be Fair Federation Transition System}


Next, we present a \textbf{restriction} $\calFF$ of the federation transition system $\calF$ and prove it to be fair (mathematically, it is a restriction of the allowed transitions of $\calF$; correspondingly, it can be thought of as a protocol describing how to fix the assemblies of a federation given changes in the federation).
Indeed, it is a restriction in the sense that the nondeterministic choices in $\calF$ of the person to populate an assembly are restricted  in  $\calFF$ to best amend under-participation of people and under-representation of child communities. 

For the description of the restriction, it is useful to measure the participation of people and  representation of child communities as follows:
Let $r$ be a run, $f$ a community that occurs in $r$ and $x$ a member or a child community of $f$ in $r$.
Then, the participation/representation measure of $x$ in $f$ at time $t$ is:
\[
\mu_t(x,f) := \frac{\bar\sigma_t(x,f)}{\bar\omega_t(x,f)}
\]
The time $t$ is omitted from $\mu_t$ when determined by the context.  Note that $\mu<1$ denotes under-participation/under-representation and $\mu>1$ denotes over-participation/over-representation.

\begin{remark}
%
Essentially, the following is a greedy protocol:  When injustice needs to be fixed, it picks the child community or the person that suffers from the most drastic injustice and chooses it to populate the assembly. The analysis then shows that, eventually, this careful greedy behavior causes the injustices to even out, leading to eventual fairness, as needed.
%
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Would-be Fair Federation Transition System]
The would-be fair federation transition system  $\calF\calF =(\calG,G_0,T)$  is obtained from the federation transition system $\calF$ by incorporating restrictions on some of its transitions as follows, with $t$ denoting the present time (note that we restrict only transitions 5 and 6): 
%
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{4}
    \item \textbf{Remove $p$ from Assembly $f$:} 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{rule-remove-tied} \textbf{Remove excessive tied representative:} If $|A_f(v)| >  \floor{\omega(v,f)}$ for some $v\in \children(f)$ then remove from $A_f$ the member $(p,v)$ with maximal $\mu(p,f)$, resolving ties lexicographically. 
        \item \label{rule-remove-floating} \textbf{Remove excessive floating representative:} Else, if  $|A_f|>n$ then remove from $A_f$ the member $(p,\bot)$ with maximal $\mu(p,f)$, resolving ties lexicographically.

        \item \textbf{Rotate representative:} \label{rule-rotate-representative}  Else, if $(p,v_p)\in A_{t',f}$ for every $t' \in [t-\Delta, t)$, then remove $(p,v_p)$ from $A_f$.  
    \end{enumerate}

    \item  \textbf{Add $p$ to Assembly of $f$:}
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \label{rule-fill-tied} \textbf{Fill a tied seat:} If  $|A_f(v)|  <  \floor{\omega(v,f)}$ for some child $v$ of $f$ then add to $A_f$ the tied representative $(p,v) \notin A_f$, $p\in P_v$, that minimizes  $\mu(p,f)$, resolving ties lexicographically.  
        %provided $p\in R(f)\cap P_v$ if it is nonempty, else $p\in P_v$.

        \item \label{rule-fill-floating} \textbf{Fill a floating seat:}  Else, if $|A_f|<n$ then add to $A_f$ the floating representative $(p,\bot)\notin A_f$, $p\in P_f$, that minimizes  $\mu(p,f)$, resolving ties lexicographically. %provided $p\in R(f)$ if it is nonempty, else $p\in P_f$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
Furthermore, recall that transitions \ref{remove-transition}, \ref{add-transition}, and \ref{gc-transition} take priority in that other transitions can be taken only if neither of them are enabled,  and they take zero time $t=0$. 
%
\end{definition}

Note that in $\calF\calF$ representatives can't just ``quit'' an assembly: They are removed if the child community they are tied to leaves (Rule \ref{rule-remove-floating}), or if their term ends (Rule \ref{rule-rotate-representative}).
A person can in fact quit the entire federation simply by removing its own ``community'' from the graph by leaving all communities they are a member of using Rule \ref{leave-transition}; garbage collection (Rule \ref{gc-transition}) will then remove the person from any assemblies they are a member of.
Also note that Rule \ref{rule-remove-tied} takes care of excess representation of a child community after another child enters a community.

Next, we show that the would-be fair federation transition system is indeed fair.
To do so, we make the technical restriction that the size of the population of non-leaf child communities is at least  $n+1$. Namely, a community must grow to at least $n+1$ before joining another as a child community, and must leave its parent community if its population shrinks below $n + 1$.  This restriction can easily be ``baked into'' the definition of $\calF\calF$. We assume this is the case (and discuss relaxing this restriction in Section~\ref{section:outlook}).

\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:would-be-is-fair}
The would-be fair federation transition system $\calF\calF$ is fair.
\end{theorem}
To prove Theorem~\ref{theorem:would-be-is-fair} we first state and prove several intermediate results.
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:would-be-is-persistently-fair}
$\calF\calF$ satisfies persistently-fair representation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $r$ be a run of $\calF\calF$ and assume by way of contradiction that at some time~$t$ all transitions that can be taken at zero time have been taken, but that $G_t$ does not satisfy persistently-fair representation.
Hence, there is a child $v$ of $f$ such that $|A_f(v)| < \floor{\omega(v,f)}$.  This must trigger transition rule \ref{rule-fill-tied},  which can be activated if there is at least one $p\in P_v\setminus M_f$, which must be the case since $|P_v|> |M_f|$ by assumption.  A contradiction.
\qed\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:eventualy-exactly-tied}
In an eventually-stable run $r$ of $\calF\calF$, for any child $v$ of $f$ in a suffix of $r$, $A_f$ will eventually have exactly $\floor{\omega(v,f)}$ representatives tied to $v$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $r$ be an eventually-stable run $r$ of $\calF\calF$, $v$ a child of $f$ in a a suffix of $r$.  Since $\calF\calF$ is persistently-fair by Proposition \ref{proposition:would-be-is-persistently-fair}, $A_f$ has at least $\floor{\omega(v,f)}$ representatives tied to $v$ at any federation in $r$.  If, after $t^*$, $A_f$ has exactly $\floor{\omega(v,f)}$ tied representatives, then no $\calF\calF$ transition can increase this number. So, assume by way of contradiction, that $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}|M_{t,f}(v)| >\floor{\omega_t(v,f)}$.  Hence, there is some $t\ge t^*$ for which $|M_{f}(v)|>\floor{\omega(v,f)}$.
At this time transition rule \ref{rule-remove-tied} can be applied repeatedly until 
\nimrodtemp{\nimrod{How do we know that, by applying transition rule \ref{rule-remove-tied} we do not reach a state in which $|M_{f}(v)| < \floor{\omega(v,f)}$?}\udi{Since we apply it only if the relation $>$ holds.  Is that OK?}\nimrod{I hope so ;) Will read again..}} $|M_{f}(v)|=\floor{\omega(v,f)}$, upon which $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}|M_{t,f}(v)| =\floor{\omega_t(v,f)}$.  A contradiction.
\qed\end{proof}

From Lemma \ref{lemma:eventualy-exactly-tied} we can conclude:

\begin{corollary}\label{corollary:exact representation}
In an eventually-stable run $r$ of $\calF\calF$, if $f$ has no floating seats in a suffix of $r$ then eventually every child $v\in \children(f)$ in $r$ will have have exact representation, namely  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\mu(v,f)=1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
In such a suffix $\sigma_t(v,f)=\floor{\omega_t(v,f)} =\omega_t(v,f)$.
\end{proof}


\begin{observation}\label{observation:one-limit}
Given a community $f$ in a suffix of an eventually-stable run $r$ of $\calF\calF$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\mu(p,f)<1$ for for some $p\in P_f$ in $r$ then $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\mu(q,f)>1$  for some  $q\ne p\in P_f$ in $r$ , and vice versa.
    \item If  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\mu(v,f)<1$ for for some child $v\in \children(f)$ in $r$ then  $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty}\mu(u,f)>1$ for some child
    $u\ne v\in \children(f)$ in $r$, and vice versa.
\end{enumerate}
\end{observation}
\begin{proof}
Informally, if all members of $P_f$ are under-represented in the limit then the assembly of $f$ must have empty seats infinitely often, a contradiction. 
Similarly, if all child communities of $f$ are under-represented in the limit then the assembly of $f$ must have empty floating seats (see Corollary~\ref{corollary:exact representation}) infinitely often, a contradiction. 
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:would-be-is-eventually-equitable}
An eventually-stable run of the would-be fair federation transition system $\calF\calF$ satisfies eventually-equitable participation. 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}

We consider an eventually-stable run $r$ with FST $t^*\ge 0$ and a community $f\in G_{t^*}$; when $G$ is omitted it is assumed to be $G_{t^*}$ and when time is omitted it is assumed to be $t^*$.
The proof is by induction on the depth (height) of $f$ in $G$.  

If $f$ is of depth $1$, then it has a single person and satisfies eventually-equitable participation and eventually-fair representation.

Inductively assume that communities of depth $d$ satisfy eventually equitable participation, which, due to Theorem \ref{theorem:equitable-implies-fair} implies that they also satisfy eventually-fair representation.
We prove the inductive claim for a community $f$ of depth $d+1$.

By way of contradiction, assume there are people in $P_f$ that satisfy eventually-equitable participation in every child community of $f$ they are members of, but not in $f$.
Due to Observation \ref{observation:one-limit}, some of them enjoy over-participation in $A_f$ the limit and some suffer under-participation in $A_f$ in the limit.

Let $U_f$ be the set of most such under-participating persons in $P_f$, namely: 
\[
(1)~~  U_f:= \arg \min_{p\in P_f} \lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \mu_t(p,f),
\] 

We first argue that either $P_v \cap U_f = \emptyset$ or $P_v \subseteq U_f$.
Assume by way of contradiction that neither is true and let $U_v := U_f\cap P_v$.  Then by assumption
$\emptyset \subset U_v\subset P_v$.  Since $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \mu_t(p,f) =c <1$ for every $p\in U_v$ and $\lim_{t\rightarrow \infty} \mu_t(q,f) > c$ for every $q\in P_v\setminus U_v$,  
there is some $t\ge t^*$ and $\epsilon>0$ s.t.  
$\mu_t(p,f) < c + \epsilon$ for every $p\in U_v$ and 
$\mu_t(q,f) \ge c + \epsilon$ for every  $q\in P_v\setminus U_v$.
Hence, for an infinite suffix of the run starting from $t$, members of $U_v$ will be preferred over members of $P_v\setminus U_v$ in filling seats in $A_f$, either tied to $v$ or floating, causing members of $U_v$ to be over-represented in $A_f$ compared to members of $P_v\setminus U_v$, a contradiction.

What this means is that if a child community $v\in\children(f)$ has one most under-represented member in $A_f$ the limit, then all members of $v$ must be most under-represented in $A_f$ the limit.

Due to Observation \ref{observation:one-limit}, not all child communities of $f$ can be most under-represented in $A_f$ in the limit, only some.  And this can only happen only if there are floating seats in $A_f$:  If all seats are tied, then according to Corollary~\ref{corollary:exact representation} each child community is perfectly represented in the parent community.  Hence assume there are floating seats, and let $u_f \subset \children(f)$ be the set of most under-represented child communities. 

By the same reasoning, 
there is some $t\ge t^*$ and $\epsilon>0$ s.t.  
$\mu_t(p,f) < c + \epsilon$ for every $p\in u\in u_f$ and 
$\mu_t(q,f) \ge c + \epsilon$ for every  $q\in u \in  \children(f) \setminus u_f$.
Hence, for an infinite suffix of the run starting from $t$, members of $u_f$ will be preferred over members of $\children(f) \setminus u_f$  in filling floating seats in $A_f$, causing communities in $u_f$ to be over-represented in $A_f$ compared to communities in $\children(f) \setminus u_f$, a contradiction.
\qed\end{proof}


\begin{proof}[of Theorem \ref{theorem:would-be-is-fair}]
According to Proposition \ref{proposition:would-be-is-persistently-fair},  $\calF\calF$ satisfies persistently-fair representation.
According to Proposition \ref{proposition:would-be-is-eventually-equitable}, $\calF\calF$ satisfies eventually-equitable participation.
According to Theorem \ref{theorem:equitable-implies-fair}, this implies that $\calF\calF$ satisfies eventually-fair representation. Hence $\calF\calF$ is fair.
\qed\end{proof}

\section{Upward Mobility}\label{section:pragmatics}


As in the previous work on this topic~\cite{halpern2024federated}, we would like the system to satisfy \emph{upward-mobility}, 
where members of a higher assembly are selected among members of the lower assembly.  Upward mobility has two goals:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Experience:}  Choose to higher assemblies seasoned members of lower assemblies.
    \item \textbf{Communication:} Enable vertical communication and the transfer of expertise and political agendas
    among higher and lower assemblies.
\end{enumerate}
In the static `one-shot' setting of~ \cite{halpern2024federated} there was only one design choice: Whether or not to require members of the higher assembly to be selected from among the members of the lower assemblies. 
Practically, doing so  allows members of a higher assembly to experience lower assembly work, and vice versa, in parallel.  This is less than ideal as the simultaneous serving in low- and high-level assemblies does not really provide for more seasoned members in high-level assemblies, since in this `one-shot' scenario they commence their two terms simultaneously. 
That work presented an algorithm that satisfied upward mobility,  the ex post, and the ex ante requirements simultaneously in the scenarios tested, but it was not proven to do so in general.

In the current dynamic setting the design-space for addressing upward mobility is much broader, and as we pioneer this direction we have no precedents to consult in choosing among the various design alternatives, only common sense. One attractive approach is the ``streaming'', or ``trickle up'', of representatives from lower assemblies to higher assemblies, by selecting a representative to a higher assembly as soon as they finish their term in the lower assembly. The sooner the better since the fresh experience and personal contacts made at the lower assembly can be brought to bear immediately at the higher assembly.  
We offer to take this approach as an extension to the protocol presented here: The next assembly member of a community is selected among the members of its population that best-satisfy the fairness requirements, with ties broken in favor of a most-recent veteran of a child assembly.  
As the pipeline of seasoned assembly members takes time to be built, and may be disrupted each time the federation structure changes, the best one can hope for is that eventually a higher assembly will consist of only seasoned members of the lower assembly, once the federation structure stabilizes.   

While highly-structured counterexamples show that achieving this precisely may not always be possible (with ``seasoned'' meaning retired less than one term ago; Daniel Halpern, personal communication), we expect simulations to show that once a federation stabilizes, and following a ``warm-up'' period, this tie-breaking rule almost always chooses members of a high-level assembly among recent (less than one term ago) veterans of the child assemblies. And we expect to prove, in future work, that in the limit there is a constant $c$ (possibly a function of the size of the assembly and the population)  after which suitable veterans can always be found after less than $c$ terms. 

The advantages of this approach is that it:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does not require overlapping membership in lower and higher assemblies.
    \item Feeds seasoned representatives from lower to higher assemblies.
    \item Enables vertical communication through personal contacts among lower and higher assembly members.
\end{enumerate}

