\section{Outlook}\label{section:outlook}

We have described Grassroots Federation and theoretically analyzed its dynamics. Crucially, we have shown a protocol that achieves relevant fairness properties and, thus, enables the grassroots formation and the fair democratic governance of large-scale, decentralized, sovereign digital communities. Here we discuss some avenues for future research.

\begin{itemize}

\item
\textbf{Simulations}: 
%
While the theoretical results show that fairness is satisfied in the limit, computer-based simulations may shed more light on the speed of convergence achieved in different situations. In particular, we conjecture that fairness is achieved quite fast for reasonable situations.

\item
\textbf{Limitations}: 
%
We have made several assumptions, such as the requirement of an upper bound on the number of children of a federation. Removing these requirements is a natural direction. Note that some of these are immediate to remove (e.g., the restriction that all assemblies are of the same size that was added for simplicity only; indeed, the grassroots nature makes changing this immediate -- practically, each federation could have their own constitution with their individual assembly size).

\item
\textbf{Grassroots proof}:  A similar, yet simpler, `direct democracy' Grassroots Federation protocol was already shown to be grassroots~\cite{shapiro2025atomic}.  We believe the proof carries over to the `assembly-based' protocol presented here; future work should turn this belief into knowledge.

\item 
\textbf{Sybil-repelling grassroots federation}:  The work on sybil-repelling community formation~\cite{poupko2021building,shahaf2020genuine} should be extended and applied to the grassroots federation of communities.
\end{itemize}


\section{Epilogue: Global Digital Democracy}\label{section:epilogue}
The grassroots research project, carried out by the authors of this paper together with the team-members and colleagues referenced herein, began in 2017 when the authors teamed with Primavera De Filippi, Raffaele Marchetti,  Liav Orgad, and Ariel Procaccia and submitted a proposal to the Global Challenges Foundation, titled \emph{Global Cloud Democracy for World Governance by Global Citizens}.  

The proposal (which ended up not being funded) concluded with a long-term vision:
\emph{We believe the only viable solution to world governance is to empower the people of the world to govern it. [...] we envision a global Cloud Democracy governed by the people, employing democracy across the local-global spectrum. [...]  The vision of global democratic governance can only be realized with the Internet as the medium for registration, deliberation, decision making, and monitoring of global and international public policies.}

While we believed the vision of global democracy to be valid~\cite{shapiro2018foundations,shapiro2018global}, the grassroots project seemingly went astray, focusing on small-scale digital democracy:  On how can a digital community form and grow without letting sybils in~\cite{poupko2021building,shahaf2020genuine}, how can it be governed democratically despite bounded sybil penetration~\cite{shahaf2019sybil,meir2024safe}, and how can its democratic processes be truly egalitarian~\cite{abramowitz2021beginning,bulteau2021aggregation,elkind2021united,abramowitz2021democratic}.  Searching for a platform upon which to realize our approach to democratically-governed digital communities, we ruled out centralized system: While harboring immense digital communities, they are under autocratic control~\cite{zuboff2019age,zuboff2022surveillance} (1 person -- all votes, see  Figure \ref{figure:economist}) and hence clearly unsuitable as a foundation for digital democracy. We intended to realize our approach on the emerging platforms for Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs)~\cite{ethereum:dao,hassan2021decentralized,taulli2022decentralized}---blockchain consensus protocols~\cite{nakamoto2008peer}, cryptocurrencies~\cite{ethereum}, and smart contracts~\cite{de2021smart}.  However, we soon realized that these platforms are fundamentally plutocratic~\cite{vitalikplutocracy} (1 coin -- 1 vote), and therefore just as unsuitable a foundation for digital democracy.  We thus began a quest for a new architecture to support egalitarian, democratic (1 person --1 vote) digital systems, within the foundations of distributed computing~\cite{shapiro2021multiagent}.  The result was the notion of grassroots protocols and platforms~\cite{shapiro2023grassrootsBA,shapiro2025atomic}.  These led to grassroots social networks~\cite{shapiro2023gsn}, grassroots cryptocurrencies~\cite{shapiro2024gc,lewis2023grassroots}, grassroots consensus~\cite{keidar2025grassroots}, and, building on earlier work in collaboration with Daniel Halpern and Ariel Procaccia~\cite{halpern2024federated}, to the current work on Grassroots Federation,  bringing us full-circle to where this project started almost a decade ago: global digital democracy.

