\subsubsection{Number of subgraphs}
We evaluated subgraphs ranging from 2 to 6, using random combinations of sampling methods for two or three subgraphs\footnote{We claim that when the number of subgraphs above 4, additional subgraph structures are constructed by different parameters (e.g. different $k$ in KNN) to prevent the over-reliance on redundant topologies.} . Performance peaked with four subgraphs, offering the best balance of effectiveness and computational efficiency in Table~\ref{tab:sensi_subgraph}. While additional subgraphs slightly improved performance, the gains were marginal and incurred higher computational costs. Therefore, we chose four subgraphs as optimal for both datasets.

