\section{Conclusion}
%\hashimoto{conclusion is too long as this is a letter article. shorten to the half.}
% An 80-MeV proton irradiation experiment was conducted at RASiS, Tohoku University, to evaluate the proposed system. The experiment confirmed that the system successfully performed both PLL evaluation and event building.
% During the experiment, the PLL operated at 540.004 MHz in approximately 54\% of the measurements. However, achieving 1 Hz accuracy in a radiation environment proved to be challenging, necessitating post-processing for precise time synchronization with the detector. 
% Furthermore, event building was successfully performed by determining the reset offset required to align all soft error events with hit events of two detectors. Through detailed analysis, the reset offset was identified as \(-32.3556 \ \mathrm{\mu s}\), ensuring precise temporal correlation between soft errors and radiation-induced events. In addition to this temporal alignment, spatial synchronization between the detection systems was achieved within a standard error of a few tens of micrometers, further confirming the robustness of the event correlation.
% In conclusion, the proposed measurement system successfully demonstrated its ability to monitor PLL operation and reproduce soft error events. These results validate the system’s effectiveness in achieving precise time and spatial synchronization and reliable event correlation, providing a foundation for further advancements in radiation-induced soft error characterization.
This paper proposes an event-wise soft-error measurement system that integrates particle detectors with a newly developed SRAM chip capable of scanning all data in 128 cycles at a clock frequency of 540 to 1024 MHz.
An 80-MeV proton irradiation experiment  verified that the system can perform sub-ns timing syncronization and event building even while the PLL is unlocked due to radiation. Event building was achieved by determining a reset offset of -32.3556 $\mathrm{\mu}s$ to align soft error and detector hit events, while spatial synchronization was maintained within a few tens of micrometers. 

