\begin{abstract}
Recent advancements in parallel simulation and successful robotic applications are spurring a resurgence in sampling-based model predictive control. To build on this progress, however, the robotics community needs common tooling for prototyping, evaluating, and deploying sampling-based controllers. We introduce \verb|judo|, a software package designed to address this need. To facilitate rapid prototyping and evaluation, \verb|judo| provides robust implementations of common sampling-based MPC algorithms and standardized benchmark tasks. It further emphasizes usability with simple but extensible interfaces for controller and task definitions, asynchronous execution for straightforward simulation-to-hardware transfer, and a highly customizable interactive GUI for tuning controllers interactively. While written in Python, the software leverages MuJoCo as its physics backend to achieve real-time performance, which we validate across both consumer and server-grade hardware. Code at \href{https://github.com/bdaiinstitute/judo}{https://github.com/bdaiinstitute/judo}.
% Sampling-based model predictive control (MPC) has seen a recent resurgence of interest, driven by advancements in parallel and hardware-accelerated simulation, as well as successful hardware demonstrations of these controllers for quadrupedal and bipedal locomotion, as well as dexterous manipulation. To build on this progress, however, the robotics community needs common tooling for prototyping, evaluating, and deploying sampling-based controllers. This paper introduces \verb|judo|, a software package that provides standardized implementations and benchmarks to enable rapid prototyping and evaluation of new algorithms, and simple, clean interfaces for developing new tasks and hardware deployments. \verb|judo| prioritizes usability, offering a simple but extensible control interface, asynchronous communication so transferring from simulation to hardware is easy, and a highly-customizable interactive GUI that lets users quickly tune and study their controllers and tasks. At the same time, \verb|judo| retains real-time performance by building on top of MuJoCo for its simulation and modeling backend, which we validate by comparing \verb|judo|'s runtime on a consumer laptop versus a server-grade CPU. Link: \red{[LINK].}
% Bolstered by advances in parallel simulation, sampling-based model predictive control (MPC) methods have recently gained more popularity for deployment in complex real-world systems, even with contact-rich dynamics. To facilitate research on and comparison of such algorithms, a standard interface is needed for rapidly prototyping, tuning, and visualizing control strategies on a wide variety of tasks. Towards that goal, this work presents \verb|judo|, a user-friendly open-source Python package for sampling-based MPC. \verb|judo| prioritizes usability, offering a simple but extensible control interface, asynchronous communication so transferring from simulation to hardware is easy, and a highly-customizable interactive GUI that lets users quickly tune and study their controllers and tasks. At the same time, \verb|judo| retains real-time performance by building on top of MuJoCo for its simulation and modeling backend, which we validate by comparing \verb|judo|'s runtime on a consumer laptop versus a server-grade CPU. Link: \red{[LINK].}

\end{abstract}

