\subsection{Full Fine-tuning on a Budget}
\label{subsec:finetuning}

In \Cref{subsec:optimizers,subsec:sensitivity,subsec:hypers-ggle,subsec:larger-models}, we studied pretraining across different model scales, always training from scratch for a single epoch. In \Cref{fig_finetuning}, we show that our findings extend to the fine-tuning setting, even when training for multiple epochs.

Because memory can often be a constraint when fine-tuning large models (more on this in \Cref{subsec:memory-usage}), it is a common practice in fine-tuning to freeze the model weights and only train Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) \citep{lora} modules, which reduces the number of trainable parameters and thus also reduces the required memory for training.

In \Cref{fig_finetuning}, we observe that Adafactor with batch size 1 performs comparably to Adam, while having a similar memory footprint to LoRA. We also see that Adam with batch size 1 and hyperparameter scaling from \Cref{eq:scalingrule} performs at least as well as Adam with batch size 16 in the fine-tuning setting too.

LoRA, however, performs noticeably worse than full parameter fine-tuning, which has also been observed in prior works \citep{lora_learns_less,lora_vs_fft}. For this reason, we argue that in a memory-constrained setting, instead of using LoRA, it is likely preferable to perform full fine-tuning with a small batch size, which obviates the need to store accumulated gradients and enables the use of memory-efficient optimizers like Adafactor without losing performance.

\section{Practical Recommendations}
\label{sec:practical}

\subsection{Memory Usage}
\label{subsec:memory-usage}

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.5\textwidth}
  \begin{center}
  \vskip -6mm
    \includegraphics[width=0.46\textwidth]{plots/memory_optimizers.pdf}
  \end{center}
  \caption{\textbf{Minimum memory requirements} to train GPT-3 (13.0B), assuming all parameters and activations are stored in 16-bit precision and gradient checkpointing is used after every transformer block layer. The dashed line shows the available memory on an NVIDIA A100 40GB GPU, which is not large enough to store expensive optimizer states or accumulated gradients. We measured the true memory usage of each optimizer by performing fused backward passes in PyTorch on an NVIDIA B200 GPU.}
  \label{fig_memory}
  \vskip -6mm
\end{wrapfigure}

Frontier LLMs are typically trained on large clusters of GPUs that necessitate large batch sizes, and, by extension, also sophisticated optimizers. However, when pretraining, and especially fine-tuning, on a single device, memory can easily become a bottleneck. We need to store the model, the optimizer state, the forward-pass activations, and optionally also accumulated gradients in memory.

We illustrate this point in \Cref{fig_memory} using the larger GPT-3 (13.0B) model as an example. Assume we store all values in bfloat16 \citep{bf16} precision and checkpoint gradients after every transformer block layer, so that we only need to store $D$ activation values per layer per token \citep{scaling-book}. Further assume that the backward pass is fused with the optimizer update step, so that the full gradient never needs to be materialized in memory. Under this setting, attempting to replicate the common practice of using Adam with a large (accumulated) batch size is infeasible on any consumer-level GPU. In contrast, it is completely feasible to train the model on a consumer-level GPU when using SGD, which is stateless, or Adafactor, which has a negligible state size. Crucially, as shown in \Cref{subsec:optimizers,subsec:sensitivity,subsec:hypers-ggle,subsec:larger-models,subsec:finetuning}, these simple optimizers achieve the best performance with small batch sizes, matching or even outperforming more sophisticated optimizers using large batch sizes.

\begin{mybox}
     \textbf{Summary}: We recommend that practitioners training large models in memory-constrained settings exploit the benefits of small batch sizes rather than trying to emulate the large batch size setting (e.g., through gradient accumulation) typically used in industry.
\end{mybox}

\subsection{Hardware Utilization}
\label{subsec:model-flops-util}

Our general recommendation for setting the batch size is to \textbf{use the smallest batch size that maximizes model throughput} (measured in tokens / second) or equivalently the model FLOPs utilization (MFU) \cite{palm}. To achieve this objective, we must avoid being bottlenecked by memory bandwidth, which in practice means using a batch size of at least several hundred tokens per device \citep{scaling-book}. Additionally, second-order optimizers like Muon introduce a fixed computational overhead per optimizer step. If the batch size becomes very small, this overhead can result in reduced model throughput. If any of these constraints become a bottleneck, we recommend increasing the batch size. 

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We show that small batch sizes, even as low as 1, consistently match or outperform large batches across model scales when tuned properly, in both pretraining and fine-tuning. In particular, SGD with batch size 1 performs on par with the AdamW baseline for GPT-3 (1.3B) with minimal tuning. We also propose a simple scaling rule for $\beta_2$ based on a fixed second-moment half-life in tokens, which generalizes across model scales.
Our results contradict common assumptions about large batch training and gradient accumulation. We recommend using the smallest batch size that maximizes throughput, avoiding gradient accumulation, and relying on simpler optimizers when memory is constrained.

Several open questions remain: How do these findings interact with batch size schedules? Can we design more effective optimizers with small state, tuned to the small batch size regime? And can we theoretically understand why fixing second-moment half-life in tokens generalizes across scales?

