\section{Problem Formulation}
\begin{comment}
Similarly to \cite{Alam_2025}, we seek to create a framework that simultaneously optimizes network capacity and \ac{TN} energy consumption by adjusting resource allocation according to network load while satisfying the data-rate requirements of each \ac{UE}. 
Specifically, our study aims to find the optimal configuration (or arm) among a set of parameters $\theta$ ($n$ different arms possible), which directly affect the network through a designed heuristic (detailed in Sec. \ref{sec:UtilityOptimization}).
To assess network performance at time $t$, we devise a cost function that captures the key trade-offs involved for a selected arm $a_t^\theta$, sampled accordingly to a distribution $x_t$ over the action space:

\begin{equation}\label{reward_function}
    f_t\left( a_t^\theta \right) = \tau \sum_{j\in\mathcal{B}} Q_j(p_j) - \sum\limits_{i \in \mathcal{U}} \log(R_i),
\end{equation}
where $R_i$ is the perceived throughput for \ac{UE} $i$, and $\tau$ acts as a regularization factor, enabling us to balance the trade-off between \ac{UE} performance (higher \ac{SLT}) and network energy consumption.
%Indeed , by maximizing the \ac{SLT} for each \ac{UE}, we ensure proportional fairness, as the logarithmic function limits excessive resource usage by any individual \ac{UE}.
Without any loss of generality, the cost function values can be normalized between $0$ and $1$.
Similarly, we define the incurred constraint violation for an arm $a_t^\theta$ as:
\begin{equation}\label{violation_function}
    g_t\left(  a_t^\theta \right) = \frac{\sum\limits_{i \in \mathcal{U}} \mathbbm{1}_{\{  R_i < \rho_i \}}}{K}.
\end{equation}
