Naturally, the cost distribution for each arm evolves over time, as the demand of the network changes with the traffic load. Indeed, an arm that proves effective in enhancing network capacity during periods of high traffic may offer limited benefit during low-traffic conditions, highlighting the importance of context-aware arm selection.
The non-stationarity of the cost over the arms aligns seamlessly with the adversarial bandit framework proposed in \cite{TAREQ PAPER}. Therefore, we can directly leverage the algorithm proposed in \cite{TAREQ PAPER} to effectively adapt to fluctuating costs while maintaining long-term constraint satisfaction.\newline
We denote by $x^*$ the "oracle" policy, i.e. the action sequence, which achieves the minimum possible cumulative loss over the time horizon:
\begin{equation}\label{oracle_policy}
\left\{ x_t^\star \right\}_{t=1}^T \in 
\arg \min_{\left\{ x_t \right\}_{t=1}^T} 
\left\{ \sum_{t=1}^T f_t(a_t^\theta) \right\}.
\end{equation}
Then, we aim to find the optimal policy $\pi$ which minimizes the cumulative loss relative to the oracle and subject to time-varying constraints. Adopting the notation from \cite{TAREQ_PAPER}, we can define the utility functions that we want to optimize as:

\begin{equation}\label{regrets_cost}
\mathfrak{R}_T(\pi) \triangleq \mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^T f_t(a_t^\theta) \right] 
- \min_{\{x_t^\star\}_{t=1}^T} \sum_{t=1}^T f_t(a_t^\theta),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}\label{regrets_violation}
\mathfrak{V}_T(\pi) \triangleq \mathbb{E}_{\pi} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^T g_t(a_t^\theta) \right]. 
\end{equation}
